WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.360
Today I have the pleasure of speaking with Patrick&nbsp;
Collison, CEO of Stripe. Patrick, first question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.920
You have an excellent compilation of advice on&nbsp;
your blog for people 10 to 20. You say there, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.400
once you turn 35, you'll write some for people&nbsp;
in their 20s. What advice do you have for us now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:09.600
the people in our 20s? When is it coming?
Patrick Collison 00:22&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.520
I haven't really thought about that. The&nbsp;
one piece of advice I've been wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:23.720
about recently is this: I said that people&nbsp;
in their teens should go to San Francisco.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:31.160
I wonder if people in their 20s shouldn't go to&nbsp;
San Francisco. That advice was a generalization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:40.680
There's a significant set of people who should&nbsp;
go to San Francisco. But there is a set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:51.160
career paths that people ought to pursue and&nbsp;
would derive most fulfillment from pursuing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:02:02.280
that are also really valuable for the world,&nbsp;
that require accumulating a lot of expertise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:11.960
and studying a domain in tremendous depth.
I think San Francisco valorizes – this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:17.800
is also San Francisco's great virtue. San&nbsp;
Francisco valorizes striking out on your own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:28.160
iconoclastically dismissing the received wisdom.&nbsp;
It praises the founding archetypes and lore of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.120
Steve Jobs and Bill Gates and all the rest.&nbsp;
I'm way less successful than those people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:39.760
but to some extent, Stripe, in as much as it fits&nbsp;
a pattern, is an instance of that pattern. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:49.800
great, I'm happy that this phenomenon exists&nbsp;
in the world. But the world needs lots of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:55.960
things. And I don't think San Francisco,&nbsp;
using San Francisco as a kind of a metonym&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:10.040
for a cultural orientation, encourages the&nbsp;
pursuit of really deep technical knowledge.&nbsp;

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:18.840
We're recording this in South San Francisco,&nbsp;
which is most noteworthy in the corporate world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:27.400
for being the headquarters of Genentech.&nbsp;
Genentech was co-founded by Bob Swanson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:35.480
and Herb Boyer. They produced cheap insulin&nbsp;
for the first time with recombinant DNA. Like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:41.280
Herb Boyer couldn't have done that at age 23.&nbsp;
Herb Boyer first had to accumulate all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:47.360
knowledge and the skills required to be able to&nbsp;
invent that over the course of a multi-decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:52.960
career. I don't know what age he was when he&nbsp;
finally went and invented it, but he was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:04:02.920
in his 20s. I feel like San Francisco doesn't&nbsp;
culturally encourage one to become Herb Boyer.&nbsp;

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.480
Or yesterday, at the time of recording this&nbsp;
podcast, Patrick Hsu, one of the co-founders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.960
of Arc, which maybe we'll speak about later&nbsp;
in the show. This is a biomedical research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:22.160
organization we started a few years ago. He&nbsp;
announced this new phenomenon of bridge editing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:31.040
which is a new recombinase where you can insert&nbsp;
DNA into a genome. It's pretty early, but it might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:38.960
turn out to be quite consequential. In order to&nbsp;
do something like that, you have to study for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:48.480
long time and acquire a lot of technical skills.
I don't quite know how to synthesize it yet, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:55.840
as I think about advice for people in their 20s,&nbsp;
I'm not going to normatively pretend to know or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:03.160
presume in which direction one should go in&nbsp;
life. Obviously, there are successful examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:08.040
of basically every strategy. I'm really glad&nbsp;
that you're doing what you're doing at what age?&nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.880
23.
23. So that's...&nbsp;

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:18.640
A podcast, I’ve got a podcast.
I think information dissemination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:27.800
is a really valuable thing in the&nbsp;
world. The guy, who, last time I heard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:39.320
was in the lead for Nat's Scroll Prize, learned&nbsp;
about it, listening to your podcast. Increasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:43.840
the catalytic surface area of certain kinds of&nbsp;
information is a valuable thing in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.240
so I'm very glad you're doing the podcast.
Anyway, I don't presume to know what people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:55.120
should do with their lives. But in as much as I&nbsp;
was trying to give advice, especially if they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:00.520
reading my advice and not someone else's, maybe&nbsp;
they're thinking about career paths that look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:06.440
directionally like mine, I think my advice might&nbsp;
be: "Maybe you should do something like what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:12.400
did or I'm trying to do. But there are other paths&nbsp;
as well. A lot of really important inventions in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:19.200
the world and a lot of the things that I'm most&nbsp;
happy are happening, require a very different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:25.240
trajectory from mine. There are counterfactual&nbsp;
versions of my life, where I pursued that path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:32.680
and who knows how well it would have worked."
Last point is that San Francisco is very status&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:40.400
oriented. Everything is status oriented, so the&nbsp;
previous statement is kind of tautological. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:50.080
feel like in San Francisco the entrepreneurs&nbsp;
are held in excessively high regard. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:07:03.640
I like entrepreneurs as a group in the world. All&nbsp;
the companies built in Stripe I think are great.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:07.800
But there's a strange emphasis placed&nbsp;
on entrepreneurship in San Francisco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:16.280
that should not be people's only fixation.
What I like about this and what I admire about you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:19.760
is that you have this sense of contrarianism – the&nbsp;
way you often challenge what people are expecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:26.960
to hear from you in a given moment. You just&nbsp;
really want to tell them the opposite. When EA was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.640
a little more popular, you were talking about the&nbsp;
important problems, and when it was down in its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:36.160
depths, you were like: "Hey guys, pay attention."&nbsp;
But on this particular piece of advice…&nbsp;

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:43.920
Michael Nielsen says that every field in science&nbsp;
has way too many adherents or way too few. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:50.520
market is almost never in the right equilibrium&nbsp;
and I think something like that might be the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.520 --> 00:08:01.040
for EA. I think reflexive contrarianism for&nbsp;
the sake of it is also tired. If you're just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:05.960
contrarian to the prevailing mood, then you're&nbsp;
following the prevailing mood but with a sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:13.200
bit inversion. I don't endorse that either.
The herd is a really powerful phenomenon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:19.440
One of the learnings of my adult life has&nbsp;
been something, that everyone knows and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:24.080
says or frequently hears, that you should&nbsp;
be very wary of following the prevailing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:28.800
tides and moods and whims and everything.&nbsp;
But it's freaking hard to do in practice.&nbsp;

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:33.080
So what practically does that look like to hone&nbsp;
your craft in any of these disciplines that take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:37.720
a long time? You've spoken and tweeted about&nbsp;
some of the problems with modern universities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:43.800
Is that still the de facto path if you want&nbsp;
to be the great biologist at Arc or something?&nbsp;

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:47.469
In many domains, I don't know. For example&nbsp;
I have no facility with or experience&nbsp;

00:08:47.469 --> 00:08:59.840
with doing things in hardware, which is not&nbsp;
a small domain. If you wanted to become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:07.000
super skilled practitioner there, what's the&nbsp;
best career path? I don't know. Maybe it's to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:18.040
drop out and join SpaceX or something. I'm&nbsp;
not necessarily endorsing pursuing the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:23.640
establishment and credential oriented path.&nbsp;
People should try to find the gradient of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:32.720
maximal learning in whatever it is they care&nbsp;
most about. The question then is what that is.&nbsp;

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:41.280
For biology, not that I'm a biologist, but it is&nbsp;
very clear that in order to do really good work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:50.560
there are a lot of "bench" skills one has&nbsp;
to acquire and there is a lot of actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:10:02.440
specific knowledge. Any kind of life wasn't&nbsp;
designed with neat fundamental principles the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:07.520
way that maybe physics was. A lot of it is&nbsp;
obviously evolved and contingent and messy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:12.160
and complicated and all the rest. So there&nbsp;
is a lot of specific factual stuff to learn.&nbsp;

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:22.960
For those two reasons, there are very few&nbsp;
successful pure autodidacts in biology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:29.920
In virtually every case that I'm aware of, at&nbsp;
some point, you have to get direct experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:40.720
in and with a top lab, where you're seeing&nbsp;
how people actually do it in practice. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:46.880
also ties back to what we were discussing&nbsp;
previously: your question about the founders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:54.640
and what they learn from each other and so on.
There's an interesting book, Apprentice to Genius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.640 --> 00:11:01.720
that follows three generations of scientists.&nbsp;
So someone who mentored somebody else, who in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:12.400
turn mentored another scientist. And they're&nbsp;
all extremely successful. The book is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:17.040
description of what they all did, but also this&nbsp;
reflection on: "What is it that was transferred?".&nbsp;

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:26.960
For example, one of the most important and subtle&nbsp;
questions in science is problem selection. How do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:32.160
you choose what to work on? No one tells&nbsp;
you what to do. And you do have to answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.880
this question multiple times. With a company, in&nbsp;
some sense, you have to decide it once, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:42.320
it's an iterative process from there. Whereas in&nbsp;
science, you're frequently pursuing completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:48.720
new problems. You need to choose something that's&nbsp;
sufficiently important and hard, so that it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:56.320
be important if you succeeded, but it also&nbsp;
mustn't be so complex that progress becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:02.920
unachievable. This is what mentees learn&nbsp;
from their mentors according to the book.&nbsp;

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:11.280
Another thing the book talks about is learning&nbsp;
about high standards and what they actually are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:19.640
When I talk to people in other domains,&nbsp;
I hear very frequently, that when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:28.400
worked with X person or Y organization or in Z&nbsp;
environment, they learned what great actually is.&nbsp;

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:34.200
And that just permanently changed their sense&nbsp;
for what their own standard for their work&nbsp;

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:46.800
ought to be. So one version of what people&nbsp;
in their 20s should do is get some ideas for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:52.320
domains you're interested in, but then figure&nbsp;
out where can you learn the highest standards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:58.240
where are the highest standards embodied, and&nbsp;
where can you go and experience that first hand.&nbsp;

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:02.240
Before we get back to Stripe and Arc Institute,&nbsp;
I want to touch on the Parker study for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:09.080
second. There's a view that says: "If we&nbsp;
improve the NIH 10% or whatever percent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:13.680
are we really making a dent in the fact that ideas&nbsp;
are getting harder to find over time? And how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:19.240
of a difference do institutions make anyways? Is&nbsp;
it just about a number of researchers and how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:23.400
people in society you can put into research? It's&nbsp;
not like Singapore can get a much more effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:28.000
scientific institution that lets it compete with&nbsp;
America in science by following this approach.&nbsp;

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:31.480
What's wrong with that intuition?
Noah Smith and others have talked about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:41.960
I can't remember the term he used, something&nbsp;
like “moneyism”. He had a funny phrase. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:50.840
pertains to the presumption that there is&nbsp;
some constant elasticity between investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:55.000
in some particular outcome, like building a&nbsp;
semiconductor factory in Arizona or a new bridge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.680 --> 00:14:01.440
and the outcome of the factory or the bridge.&nbsp;
First of all, the conversion rate between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:11.120
those inputs and the output is not a cosmological&nbsp;
constant. Maybe any of these things could be done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:21.240
for half or a tenth of the cost. Secondly, there&nbsp;
are even deeper questions as to, is it possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:28.080
at all? What else would have to change for it to&nbsp;
be possible? What are the other constraints? By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:31.520
talking about these things in funding and&nbsp;
dollar terms, you're making the implicit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:35.880
assumption that the only relevant constraint&nbsp;
is the financial one, where in practice, maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:45.320
it's permits or labor shortages or other things.&nbsp;
In the context of the NIH and science and R&amp;D,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:55.320
I'm really skeptical of this approach being&nbsp;
brought to bear, where we can just talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.320 --> 00:15:02.320
amount that we're spending on R&amp;D and think that&nbsp;
that's implicitly a useful measure of the output.&nbsp;

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:10.000
To a fairly close approximation, there were&nbsp;
around 1% as many practicing professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:20.200
scientists in the US pre-World War II as&nbsp;
there were post-World War II or say even 1950.&nbsp;

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:31.320
The other epiphenomena in papers or patents and so&nbsp;
forth tend to follow pretty similar ratios. We got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:41.600
a lot of pretty good stuff in the first half&nbsp;
of the century. Despite increasing the amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:51.160
that we spend between two and maybe slightly more&nbsp;
than two orders of magnitude it's not clear to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:57.440
that there is a direct linear relationship between&nbsp;
spending and output. When analyzing the NIH or how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:07.920
we should pursue any of this stuff, I try to get&nbsp;
more concrete and tactile and think, what would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:16.640
success here look like? What is happening today at&nbsp;
the microscale? What are the actual problems? What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:22.720
could success look like at the microscale?&nbsp;
What might it look like to scale that up?&nbsp;

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:30.800
One example of that, we ran a survey of the Fast&nbsp;
Grants grant recipients after Fast Grants asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:36.000
about their normal work, and not about anything to&nbsp;
do with Fast Grants itself. We asked them if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:49.240
had flexible funding, that is to say if they could&nbsp;
direct their current research dollars however they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:53.920
wanted how much their associated research program&nbsp;
would change. And we gave them three options:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.920 --> 00:17:00.760
not much, a little, and a lot. Four out of&nbsp;
five(79%) said that their research agenda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:10.000
would change a lot if this constraint was removed.
Asking: "Should the NIH funding level be X or 1.1X&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:14.160
or 1.2X or whatever?" seems to me like a bad&nbsp;
way to analyze this question as compared to:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:23.600
"How bound and constrained should an NIH grantee&nbsp;
be in choosing their research agenda?" Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:29.680
their judgment is way better than that of the&nbsp;
committees, not saying it is, but who knows?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:37.200
Maybe there's a 5X improvement to be generated&nbsp;
just by making that one switch? I'm very skeptical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:44.480
of these financially-oriented frameworks.
Maybe financial is not the right word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:49.160
for it, but just trying to map inputs to outputs&nbsp;
is the framing, which you're using to compare the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:55.840
pre-World War II inputs to what's happening&nbsp;
now. If it was particular to the scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.840 --> 00:18:00.440
institutions, you'd expect, for example, that&nbsp;
things that are disconnected from the NIH-specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:04.280
structures would show different trends. You've&nbsp;
talked a lot about the fact that it's getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:09.920
harder to find impactful papers. Sector through&nbsp;
sector, it's not like NIH is running Moore's Law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:13.520
progress, right? But even there you see that you&nbsp;
need exponentially more researchers to keep up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:19.920
same level of progress. It does seem important to&nbsp;
have these "level effects", that are one-time, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:23.680
the case of something like COVID happens, where we&nbsp;
say: "Yeah, we need that level-effect" right now."&nbsp;

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:27.040
But if we're framing it in terms of hundreds&nbsp;
of years from now, I wonder if these events are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.040 --> 00:18:32.280
going to be a thing that increases growth&nbsp;
rates, which is a sort of framing that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:36.840
also applied when talking about progress.
Does that make sense in that context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:42.520
when all sectors are seeing slowdowns,&nbsp;
which seem consistent with how the economy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.960
and science progresses over time?
“I don't know” is the short answer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:56.000
It's really puzzling. The constancy of US&nbsp;
GDP growth is one of the weirdest things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:02.760
I don't know if I've got an explanation for it.&nbsp;
An obvious thing to do would be to shrug and say:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:07.760
"OK, well, it's overdetermined, and that's just&nbsp;
how countries work." But you can look at other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:13.000
countries, where it's manifestly not the case.&nbsp;
What is it that's weird and special about the US?&nbsp;

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:19.040
The thing that I wonder about in a lot of these&nbsp;
cases is: you could get many of the observed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:27.400
system phenomenon characteristics, if we weren't&nbsp;
actually adding productive capacity. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:32.640
a simple way to explain a lot of it, in that if&nbsp;
you're just adding exponentially more unproductive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:39.760
capacity, then on a stylized level, a lot of this&nbsp;
stuff would just fall out of it. Now, I'm not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:50.600
saying that we're necessarily doing that, but it&nbsp;
could be that maybe we're making them... There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:58.200
lots of ways where that could be quite effectively&nbsp;
going on, even if it's not the case that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:03.440
marginal people or things or organizations&nbsp;
themselves are bad. It's just how the components&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:10.000
interact. But the fact that you could get these&nbsp;
exponentially diminishing returns through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:18.160
addition of ever more nonproductive capacity&nbsp;
makes me not persuaded that the low-hanging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:26.040
case is necessarily true, and gives some&nbsp;
weight to the prospect that it's fundamentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:37.360
structural, cultural, or organizational.
Just to give a micro example there, and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:48.520
a very basic and an obvious one, it's interesting&nbsp;
to compare the SpaceX R&amp;D budget and the NASA R&amp;D&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:59.320
budget and to actually look at those two time&nbsp;
series together. Maybe we're just returning to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:13.480
the financial point again, but it seems pretty&nbsp;
clear that the trajectory of NASA's efficacy has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:20.320
not fully followed the trajectory of its inputs.
Yeah, although the point about: "the marginal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:26.160
inputs we put into science have not been&nbsp;
as effectively used as what was before".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:31.840
The 1X then was a 1X of much higher quality, than&nbsp;
100X now. It's not clear what you do to fix that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:34.960
If it's just a case, that there's a limited&nbsp;
amount of John von Neumanns in your society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:40.320
that are part of the pre-World War II 1X,&nbsp;
it's not like we can just put 100X more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:45.560
John von Neumann-type physicists into science.
If the binding constraint is the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:51.240
John von Neumann's, then yes, that's bad news, I&nbsp;
guess. There's not a lot we can do on the margin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.240 --> 00:22:00.000
But I'm not sure that it is. I keep going back&nbsp;
to the cultural and sociological point. Gerty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:07.320
and Carl Cori, they ran a lab at the University of&nbsp;
Washington, St. Louis. And six of their students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:12.120
if I recall correctly, went on to win&nbsp;
Nobel prizes. They had a well-known lab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:17.360
they got good students, but they weren't the most&nbsp;
prestigious lab in the world. It's not like they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:24.600
got to cherry-pick every year the single most&nbsp;
promising person, so something was going on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:30.920
there. There's a book about it, which tries to&nbsp;
get into this a little bit. I don't know if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.920 --> 00:22:35.320
can figure out quite what it was. There was also&nbsp;
some good fortune, where they got into molecular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.320 --> 00:22:41.640
biology at a good time, but I think there were&nbsp;
these "hopeful data points". Again, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:46.920
extremely brilliant people, but the thing that&nbsp;
distinguished them and their students was not that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:54.960
they were these seven "sigma-Martians", it was&nbsp;
rather that they found organizational structures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:59.480
and cultural practices that really worked. Those&nbsp;
are, at least in principle, more replicable.&nbsp;

00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:02.600
Now, you might still say: "OK, fine,&nbsp;
in theory. But how do you actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:09.880
do that?" That's the big open question.
OK, that's a great point to talk about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:16.280
Arc institute. I think you just answered&nbsp;
this question but still: It's not exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:21.080
like biology research is, it's something that&nbsp;
society has neglected. So what's the theory of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:26.160
change here? Is it just a story similar to Stripe?&nbsp;
In that, if you get the right people, there's tens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:29.800
of billions of dollars of biology funding.&nbsp;
Getting the right people, the right culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:34.640
and right education is what it takes, right?
Even though there are lots of scientists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:42.200
lots of universities, there's a lot&nbsp;
of homogeneity today in how science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.200 --> 00:23:47.960
and in particular, how biomedical science&nbsp;
is pursued, where basic research is done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:55.040
in an academic context before there's any&nbsp;
commercialization prospect in sight. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:59.200
know that this model is necessarily a bad one.&nbsp;
Certainly, we're not claiming that it's a bad one.&nbsp;

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:07.480
Construct of universities, labs, PI — a principal&nbsp;
investigator running the lab, who applies for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:16.320
grants primarily to the NIH, maybe supplemented&nbsp;
by other sources, grants reviewed by committees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:23.192
with "study sections", as they call them, with&nbsp;
pretty rigid scoring criteria and so on — that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.192 --> 00:24:32.320
is the structure and it seems suboptimal to me.
Homogeneity is bad in basically any ecosystem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:42.120
especially ecosystems where you're producing&nbsp;
or seeking tail outcomes. And we thought that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:49.800
for a variety of reasons, from first principles,&nbsp;
other models should be possible. We had specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:55.760
ideas as to how one particular model might be a&nbsp;
good idea and complementary to the status quo.&nbsp;

00:24:55.760 --> 00:25:03.800
In very short terms, what's different&nbsp;
about Arc is: one, scientists are funded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:07.880
themselves to pursue whatever they want.&nbsp;
So it's curiosity-driven research, whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.880 --> 00:25:13.360
NIH grants are given for projects. Second,&nbsp;
we build a lot of in-house infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:18.640
so that scientists can draw upon other platforms&nbsp;
and capabilities that they don't have to build and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:25.560
maintain themselves. Whereas, in the standard&nbsp;
university academic context, scientists would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:30.320
virtually always have to do that in-house. Because&nbsp;
of the natural scale constraints on any given lab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:37.720
that effectively circumscribes the ambition&nbsp;
of a possible research program. And thirdly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:42.800
we try to provide career paths for people to&nbsp;
remain in science if they don't want to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:50.400
principal investigators, whereas the university&nbsp;
structure commingles the training purpose of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.440 --> 00:26:00.560
academia with the execution — the people who&nbsp;
are doing the work there are typically the grad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.560 --> 00:26:06.800
students and the postdocs, who are themselves, at&nbsp;
least nominally, on the career path of eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:13.760
becoming principal investigators. There are lots&nbsp;
of people who, for all sorts of different very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:20.680
valid reasons, love science and the pursuit of&nbsp;
research, but don't want to be a manager running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:26.280
a lab, choosing their own research programs, and&nbsp;
dealing with all of the overhead and typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:32.680
grant applications that are concomitant with that.
With Arc, we have a real emphasis on hiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:37.280
scientists to finish their postdocs, finish grad&nbsp;
school, who know that that's what they want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:41.840
do in their lives. And again, it isn't really&nbsp;
a career path for them today. One of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:45.200
that's really exciting about the discovery,&nbsp;
that we mentioned, that came out yesterday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:50.920
this new bridge editing technology, is: that&nbsp;
work was led by one of senior scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.920 --> 00:27:02.040
who had finished his postdoc. It's not clear&nbsp;
to me that he wanted to become a PI, but he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:10.120
loved science, and he's an amazing researcher,&nbsp;
so he's able to go and have that career at Arc.&nbsp;

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:21.560
In addition, the prospect of mobile elements&nbsp;
being usable in this way for genomic insertion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.560 --> 00:27:26.600
whatever, — that's a pretty speculative, out&nbsp;
there thing. Had he applied to the NIH to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:30.920
and pursue that? He didn't, so I don't&nbsp;
know what the outcome would have been.&nbsp;

00:27:30.920 --> 00:27:37.240
But Jennifer Doudna's work was, if&nbsp;
I recall correctly, funded by DARPA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:47.880
because her CRISPR NIH applications were rejected.&nbsp;
Katalin Kariko's NIH applications for mRNA vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:55.320
work were famously rejected. It at least seems&nbsp;
very plausible that it wouldn't have worked out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:02.920
All these things are random, and I can't make&nbsp;
any definitive claims about what would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:07.240
counterfactually happened. But it seems plausible&nbsp;
to me that this thing announced yesterday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:12.840
wouldn't have happened or would have been less&nbsp;
likely to happen in a different environment.&nbsp;

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:21.280
When we think forward 10 or 20 years, this&nbsp;
specific line of research, where you understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:27.200
the effects of the genetic architecture on&nbsp;
different traits, and you can edit, invert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.920 --> 00:28:34.760
insert the DNA arbitrarily. You've solved cell&nbsp;
anemia — you've done the obvious things. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:38.360
does that lead to? What are you excited about?
The thing that is really interesting about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:50.280
is using it as a new kind of telescope: when&nbsp;
people hear about CRISPR, there's an obvious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:55.360
and legitimate excitement around using this to&nbsp;
cure things directly in the body, as a kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.360 --> 00:29:03.080
therapeutic. You can also use CRISPR to try&nbsp;
to figure out what's going on in cells and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:03.080 --> 00:29:14.280
cell cultures in a structured way. So the body is&nbsp;
interesting in that it has this switchboard, akin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:25.080
to DJ’s with those fancy mixing sets, of 20,000&nbsp;
genes. And with CRISPR, you can systematically go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:25.080 --> 00:29:31.720
and perturb each gene one by one, mashing all the&nbsp;
keys in sequence, and try to figure out what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:31.720 --> 00:29:38.520
effects of perturbing this versus that are. If you&nbsp;
do that in a cell culture, where you can subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:45.720
the cells to some stressor or treatment, you can&nbsp;
see differentially how different perturbations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:52.160
affect different cell outcomes. Or you can use&nbsp;
it for synthetic data generation more broadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:55.840
where you could perform all these perturbations,&nbsp;
then sequence and see what's happening in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:55.840 --> 00:30:00.960
cells and so forth. And single cell sequencing has&nbsp;
come a long way. Anyway, the point is, there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:10.400
lot you can do with gene editing for discovery&nbsp;
and for data generation in the broadest sense.&nbsp;

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:21.600
That's really compelling, because a lot of&nbsp;
diseases are "complex" in the field's jargon. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:25.400
they're complex in the colloquial sense, but&nbsp;
they're specifically complex in that they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.400 --> 00:30:31.680
not infectious. They're not just some pathogen&nbsp;
getting into you. And they're not monogenic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:35.760
like Huntington's, where it's one specific&nbsp;
mutation. Instead, they are some combination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:40.480
of environmental factors, but maybe some&nbsp;
genetic factors as well — they are somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:40.480 --> 00:30:50.920
in between. These include most autoimmune&nbsp;
diseases, most cancers, to some extent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:50.920 --> 00:30:55.760
cardiovascular disease and neurodegenerative&nbsp;
disease — the big ones we haven't yet solved.&nbsp;

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:03.640
Coming back to functional genomics technologies,&nbsp;
what's interesting is trying to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:04.680 --> 00:31:12.680
how it is that the genetic component of those&nbsp;
diseases works. And even if that's only a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:12.680 --> 00:31:18.320
contributor, it can potentially shine light on&nbsp;
what the general pathway is. So the question would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:18.320 --> 00:31:23.080
be, and this is speculative, none of this has&nbsp;
actually happened: "By figuring out the genetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.080 --> 00:31:31.240
interactions between genes and, say, Alzheimer's,&nbsp;
can you figure out how Alzheimer's arises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.240 --> 00:31:35.840
which we don't understand today?" Then once you&nbsp;
understand how Alzheimer's arises, maybe you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:49.680
use conventional technologies to figure out how&nbsp;
to inhibit or modulate those pathways. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:54.440
what we're really excited about from a functional&nbsp;
genomics standpoint. There's an AI angle as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:54.440 --> 00:31:59.720
that we could talk about if you want.
How do you think about the dual use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.720 --> 00:32:04.760
possibilities of biotech? I am sympathetic with&nbsp;
the idea that if you think of prior technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.760 --> 00:32:09.680
like Google search or even the computer&nbsp;
itself, you could forecast in advance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.680 --> 00:32:14.680
like: "Oh, this has all this dual use stuff."&nbsp;
But for some reason, history has been kind to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:23.320
us. The meta-lesson here is: “Keep doing science.”
With biotech, we don't have to go into specifics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.320 --> 00:32:27.160
here, but are there specific things you can think&nbsp;
of with this specific technology? You can imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.160 --> 00:32:33.680
some nefarious things. How do you think about&nbsp;
that? Why not focus, let's say, on ameliorating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.680 --> 00:32:43.160
the risks first or something like that?
I don't think that the binding constraint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:52.000
on harmful use of biotechnology or bioweapons&nbsp;
today is pure biological capabilities. If some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:59.480
set of incredibly capable, intelligent people&nbsp;
wanted to cause tremendous harm, presumably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:59.480 --> 00:33:04.800
with pathogens or with something biological, they&nbsp;
wouldn't necessarily need to invent anything new.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.800 --> 00:33:10.400
They would just need to apply currently known&nbsp;
techniques in a malevolently directed fashion.&nbsp;

00:33:11.320 --> 00:33:18.160
There are some concerns and risks with respect&nbsp;
to things that don't invent new technologies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:30.320
but do make them more accessible. The question&nbsp;
is, what would the effect on the world be if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:30.320 --> 00:33:37.800
there was a sufficiently sophisticated&nbsp;
LLM that could help anybody synthesize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:43.920
and disperse smallpox? I don't know laws of&nbsp;
physics that prohibit such an LLM existing — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:50.720
presume they don't. Would the world be fine&nbsp;
if such an LLM was widely distributed? Maybe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:50.720 --> 00:33:57.840
but maybe not. So there is that threat factor,&nbsp;
but my point is: I don't think knowledge at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:57.840 --> 00:34:09.520
frontier of biology is the relevant margin here.
If we take this seriously, we don't need crazy AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.520 --> 00:34:15.480
risks to motivate this. The world is perfectly&nbsp;
capable of originating really severe pandemics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:15.480 --> 00:34:21.160
and pathogens itself, plus all the other diseases&nbsp;
that are not pathogenic. So we got other problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:22.240 --> 00:34:27.320
Whether we care about the possible dual use&nbsp;
harms you just mentioned, or we just care about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.320 --> 00:34:33.640
things that already exist, to ameliorate both of&nbsp;
those, we do need enhancement of our capabilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:36.640 --> 00:34:43.000
There are a lot of biological problems that we&nbsp;
don't know how to solve today. In that respect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:49.360
if one were to do what you're proposing and&nbsp;
try to advance the defensive side of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:49.360 --> 00:34:54.800
I don't know that, what one would do, would&nbsp;
necessarily be that different. Because there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.800 --> 00:35:02.600
are just fundamental capabilities that we would&nbsp;
presumably need to have, that we don't have today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:02.600 --> 00:35:08.840
By trying to solve current human diseases, you're&nbsp;
probably also pursuing something pretty close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:08.840 --> 00:35:14.000
the best steps to solve the potential diseases&nbsp;
that malicious actors could cause in the future.&nbsp;

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:19.400
That makes sense. Zooming out&nbsp;
from bio risk in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:28.160
how are you thinking about AI these days?
Everyone has to be highly perplexed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:35.200
in the sense that the verdict that&nbsp;
one might have given at the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:37.520
of 2023, 2021, back, say, the last eight years —&nbsp;
we're recording this pretty close to the beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:51.520
of 2024 — would have looked pretty different.
Maybe Gwern might have scored the best from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.520 --> 00:36:00.760
2019 or something onwards, but broadly speaking,&nbsp;
it's been pretty difficult to forecast. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:07.880
basic position to a first order has to be some&nbsp;
degree of humility. As your blog post identifies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:16.640
the big question right now is: "To what&nbsp;
degree scaling laws hold?" And if they hold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:23.760
then what exactly is it that we're, asymptoting is&nbsp;
maybe a presumptuous word, it's not an asymptote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:29.320
but what is it, that we're approaching? We&nbsp;
don't necessarily know the shape of that thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:29.320 --> 00:36:44.600
whatever it is. How one should feel ought to be&nbsp;
very sensitive to the exact parameters of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:51.520
curves, and I don't think anyone knows what&nbsp;
the true value of those parameters actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:51.520 --> 00:36:58.240
are. It's clearly going to be important,&nbsp;
is already important today, it has a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:58.240 --> 00:37:08.840
central bearing on both Stripe and Arc. We'll see.
I totally agree with that general sentiment but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.840 --> 00:37:13.360
wonder if the meta lesson that we got from COVID,&nbsp;
for example, and with things like Fast Grants was:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:18.480
you obviously can't predict these things&nbsp;
in advance, but the most important thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:22.440
in addition to specific countermeasures you&nbsp;
are trying to come up with in advance, is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:28.120
when the crisis is happening, having competent&nbsp;
individuals who can synthesize and organize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:28.120 --> 00:37:35.560
information, and also having new initiatives&nbsp;
and institutions to get the right thing done.&nbsp;

00:37:35.560 --> 00:37:38.720
The adaptability premium is probably&nbsp;
going to go way up over the next decade.&nbsp;

00:37:38.720 --> 00:37:44.400
Yeah. With that in mind, I know you already have&nbsp;
a couple of day jobs, but I feel like something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:50.920
similar to fast grants, when the time comes down&nbsp;
to it, should be there. You'd be one of the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:50.920 --> 00:37:54.520
people you could think of, in terms of having&nbsp;
expertise and respect in a wide range of domains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:54.520 --> 00:37:59.960
and competency as a leader. Just keep it in the&nbsp;
back of your mind, maybe in the middle of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:59.960 --> 00:38:10.120
mind, given how far we are into the transition.
Well, Fast Grants was three beloved squirrels in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.120 --> 00:38:18.280
a trench coat. I was one of the squirrels.&nbsp;
It was also Tyler Cowen, who's an amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.280 --> 00:38:23.720
person and a great friend, and then my wife,&nbsp;
who's also one of Arc's co-founders. Fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:23.720 --> 00:38:29.720
Grants was not this giant, impressive, office
that would qualify me for anything at all.&nbsp;

00:38:29.720 --> 00:38:32.400
But it isn't hard to be giant, right,&nbsp;
to have that kind of big impact.&nbsp;

00:38:34.800 --> 00:38:39.320
As an objective matter, that's true.&nbsp;
John and I try to be very self-aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:39.320 --> 00:38:48.080
of the limits of our expertise, which&nbsp;
are very proximate to us. I'm sure if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:48.760 --> 00:38:54.320
something like that was necessary, that'd be.
Look at Operation Warp Speed! They chose a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.320 --> 00:39:00.800
super effective domain expert, Moncef Slaoui,&nbsp;
to run that and it was monstrously successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:01.680 --> 00:39:06.360
truly remarkable. I don't know who&nbsp;
the Moncef Slaoui of the problem is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.360 --> 00:39:10.720
it would depend on the problem in question, but my&nbsp;
recommendation would be: "Figure out who Moncef is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.720 --> 00:39:21.840
and go hire Moncef." I think anybody who deemed&nbsp;
me the Moncef of that thing is probably mistaken.&nbsp;

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:27.960
I think you're being too humble. Staying on&nbsp;
Fast Grants, now we have the retrospective of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:32.120
how effective the fast grants recipients were,&nbsp;
compared to the other grants that were given out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.120 --> 00:39:38.440
by, let's say, the NIH or NSF. To your knowledge,&nbsp;
what has been the reaction of these institutions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.440 --> 00:39:43.320
to the discrepancy between the speed and&nbsp;
effectiveness of fast grants? Have they analyzed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:43.320 --> 00:39:49.360
their protocols and what happened during COVID?&nbsp;
Is there any retrospective there on their part?&nbsp;

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:54.520
Not to my knowledge, but I don't want that&nbsp;
to sound like an indictment. Maybe they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.520 --> 00:39:59.320
done a lot of reflection, and I just don't know&nbsp;
about it. I don't think I would know about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.320 --> 00:40:14.360
even if it had happened. So, I don't know.&nbsp;
I don't know anything about the response at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:14.360 --> 00:40:21.400
CDC or FDA or NIH or NSF or any of the relevant&nbsp;
organizations or their international equivalents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.400 --> 00:40:28.040
So what I'm saying should be taken as not only&nbsp;
not critical of them, but not even as a comment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:28.040 --> 00:40:35.760
to them. I just don't know what they did.
In general, organizations are not awesome at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:41.480
self-reflection. I assume as a default prior,&nbsp;
that some of the dynamics we discussed at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:41.480 --> 00:40:47.640
beginning of this are rooted there. None of the&nbsp;
people who started those organizations are there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:47.640 --> 00:41:05.360
today. What exactly are the incentives of those&nbsp;
leaders? It's not clear to me who would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.360 --> 00:41:14.040
the incentive to really take stock in a fully&nbsp;
objective and self-critical way, to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:14.040 --> 00:41:18.080
what was done well and what was done poorly.
I promise not to be too myopic about AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:18.080 --> 00:41:21.720
but one more question. Long-term, we&nbsp;
can't forecast. Maybe even medium-term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:21.720 --> 00:41:26.120
we can't. But near-term, it looks like we&nbsp;
might have things that look like AI agents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:32.280
and they might need to trade. What does the&nbsp;
financial infrastructure for AI agents look like?&nbsp;

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:46.480
That's a really interesting question. Automated&nbsp;
or autonomous transactions already exist to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:51.560
extent today. Lots of services have&nbsp;
usage-based billing, right? A lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:51.560 --> 00:41:58.840
the expenses being incurred are autonomously&nbsp;
incurred. No human is pushing a button when&nbsp;

00:41:58.840 --> 00:42:03.600
Stripe does most of what it does with cloud&nbsp;
computing and incurs some cost with some cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:10.120
service. In an extremely primitive way it's&nbsp;
happening today. I assume it will follow some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:10.120 --> 00:42:20.960
gradient, where some of those decisions will&nbsp;
be made by an LLM or LLM equivalent. There'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:20.960 --> 00:42:28.920
be an almost unnoticeably smooth continuum up to&nbsp;
very considerable degrees of autonomy. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:28.920 --> 00:42:34.360
that we're going to wake up some month and be&nbsp;
like: "Oh my god, suddenly the bots have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:34.360 --> 00:42:47.640
unleashed." This will now sound very parochial&nbsp;
and maybe I'm getting excessively tactical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:47.640 --> 00:42:53.000
but there'll be very interesting questions around&nbsp;
the legality of bots in terms of: are they treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:53.000 --> 00:43:00.800
as the responsibility of the owner? Is there any&nbsp;
degree of independence granted? How does liability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:07.400
work? Which rails are best suited? What kind&nbsp;
of transaction velocities are we talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:07.400 --> 00:43:11.920
here? Because if it's a billion transactions&nbsp;
a second, then the properties of that system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:17.720
should look very different to one giant tiering&nbsp;
transaction every day. If we just use the analogy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:26.080
of the usage-based services, those tend to incur&nbsp;
liabilities in tiny increments, but then to settle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:26.800 --> 00:43:32.680
on a monthly basis when you pay your bill. So&nbsp;
maybe these agent transactions will have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:32.680 --> 00:43:39.200
character. There are a lot of practical applied&nbsp;
questions, but I think what you're saying around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:39.200 --> 00:43:45.720
these autonomous transactions conceivably being an&nbsp;
important dimension, is very true and real and is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.320 --> 00:43:54.240
one of the interesting ways in which the economy&nbsp;
might change and expand over the next decade.&nbsp;

00:43:54.240 --> 00:44:07.560
It's possible that the crypto plays&nbsp;
some role here. We take KYC and AML&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:13.800
very seriously for humans: we want to know the&nbsp;
human that is associated with some particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:20.680
financial activity. Obviously, that's a murkier&nbsp;
question in the context of some AI agent. If we,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.680 --> 00:44:27.480
in some blurry sense, look at crypto as the part&nbsp;
of financial services that is de facto exempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:28.080 --> 00:44:35.480
from AML by design, then maybe that plays a role.
How long before Stripe was founded do you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:35.480 --> 00:44:45.560
a product like Stripe could have been invented?
That's a good question. Depending on what exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:45.560 --> 00:44:53.080
you define Stripe as being, conceivably&nbsp;
decades earlier in that, at some level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:53.080 --> 00:45:02.360
PayPal is a kind of Stripe. There were many&nbsp;
payment companies before PayPal. You could go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:02.360 --> 00:45:11.240
all the way back to cash registers, so it depends&nbsp;
on definitional questions. The particular secular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:16.400
tailwinds that we benefited from were tied to&nbsp;
the rise of app stores, the on-demand economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:16.400 --> 00:45:23.400
and maybe the startup boom post-YC and the&nbsp;
financial crisis; those particular tailwinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:30.280
were idiosyncratic and specific to Stripe. The&nbsp;
GFC was 2008 2009 and Stripe was founded in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.280 --> 00:45:37.040
so as much as you define those as being core,&nbsp;
then not that much earlier. Mostly my story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:37.040 --> 00:45:44.480
of Stripe is one of market inefficiency. I do&nbsp;
wonder why much of this didn't happen sooner.&nbsp;

00:45:45.480 --> 00:45:50.440
I always find it really interesting when there's&nbsp;
cases, where it wasn't even the case that: "Well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:50.440 --> 00:45:52.920
it could have been started sooner, but there was&nbsp;
nobody in the market." There were many people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:52.920 --> 00:45:55.880
the market. And they weren't just random people,&nbsp;
they were technology companies headquartered in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:55.880 --> 00:46:01.880
San Francisco who were in the market. Do you have&nbsp;
some explanation for why it didn't occur to them?&nbsp;

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:09.360
I'm hesitant to generalize too much, because&nbsp;
I only have maybe n equals 1 experience. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:09.360 --> 00:46:14.880
dangerous to over extrapolate from that. Maybe n&nbsp;
equals 2 now with Arc, as a very different kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:14.880 --> 00:46:18.840
organization, but an organization nonetheless.
Or if you include all the features of Stripe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.840 --> 00:46:22.600
n equals 10, 20 something.
OK, yes, depending on your definition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:22.600 --> 00:46:27.080
maybe there's some kind of samples out there.&nbsp;
My general view is: "For most products and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:27.080 --> 00:46:39.040
most businesses, things can just be done much&nbsp;
better". Moats are typically overrated. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:40.080 --> 00:46:44.280
payments are a great example of a domain where,&nbsp;
on a logical basis, you would say that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:44.280 --> 00:46:50.440
so many sources of defensibility: there's the&nbsp;
network effects of the account holders, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:50.440 --> 00:46:59.880
data network effects/economy of scale for fraud,&nbsp;
regulatory modes and barriers etc. etc. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:00.760 --> 00:47:04.640
not only does Stripe exist, but there are lots of&nbsp;
others. There's a whole fintech ecosystem today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:04.640 --> 00:47:14.560
right? It gets down to deep questions of: "What is&nbsp;
the binding constraint on the number of effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:18.800
organizations that exist in the world?" For any&nbsp;
given sector, why is it that number of companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:18.800 --> 00:47:25.400
rather than twice that number of companies and&nbsp;
so on? It's about motivation, ideas and people's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:25.400 --> 00:47:29.760
willingness and determination to organize&nbsp;
talent and so forth. But these are kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:37.000
of more sociocultural explanations.
Hamilton Helmer is probably the leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:37.000 --> 00:47:45.880
scholar on various sources of defensibility&nbsp;
for businesses. He has this niche, but very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:45.880 --> 00:47:54.240
well known in the niche, book called Seven&nbsp;
Powers. It tends to disaggregate the various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:54.240 --> 00:48:00.200
sources of market power in this respect. I&nbsp;
think that is true and important, insofar as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:00.200 --> 00:48:06.800
it goes. Nonetheless, it's kind of strange to&nbsp;
me that nobody had done Stripe before Stripe.&nbsp;

00:48:06.800 --> 00:48:12.040
When you think about the fact that moats are&nbsp;
overrated and just doing the thing is underrated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:12.880 --> 00:48:17.840
what is Stripe's mode in that context? Does that&nbsp;
make you think differently about Stripe's mode?&nbsp;

00:48:18.880 --> 00:48:36.800
Yes, I do think that one can have organizational&nbsp;
and cultural moats. Maybe this contradicts what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:36.800 --> 00:48:39.520
I was just saying, or it's consistent with&nbsp;
it in the sense that it's a kind of cultural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:39.520 --> 00:48:47.800
explanation. In as much as we have a moat,&nbsp;
it's because we have a very good understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:47.800 --> 00:48:52.800
of our domain. We have a set of people who&nbsp;
actually care about solving the problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:53.360 --> 00:48:58.640
who are continually paranoid at the prospect&nbsp;
that we might be forgetting something important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:58.640 --> 00:49:02.920
So we are trying to figure out what the important&nbsp;
thing that could supplant Stripe's approaches is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:02.920 --> 00:49:13.840
and make sure that we build that first.
You're familiar with Conquest's laws.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:13.840 --> 00:49:19.760
There's Conquest's third law, which is that: "One&nbsp;
should model organizations as if they're run by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:19.760 --> 00:49:27.080
cabal of their enemies." Presumably it's tongue&nbsp;
in cheek, but it's interesting to try to think:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:31.600
"Well, what is the kernel of truth in that and why&nbsp;
would it be there?" I think what's going on is:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:32.600 --> 00:49:37.440
most organizations, when they start out, are&nbsp;
actually trying to achieve their stated goals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:38.240 --> 00:49:43.840
Somebody started the organization for a reason and&nbsp;
probably it was for the stated reason. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:51.640
over time, that person and that set of people who&nbsp;
initially populated the organization depart and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:51.640 --> 00:49:54.640
some set of new people come to take their place.&nbsp;
And there's multiple iterations of that, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:56.040 --> 00:50:01.720
generational turnover on a continuous basis. But&nbsp;
say, for the fifth generation: Why are they there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:01.720 --> 00:50:08.520
and to what degree do their particular incentives&nbsp;
align with the originally stated goals of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:08.520 --> 00:50:14.120
organization? There can be a lot of misalignment&nbsp;
there, where they're following a local path,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:15.600 --> 00:50:20.920
conceivably even the leader of the organization&nbsp;
does it, not necessarily through any fault of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:20.920 --> 00:50:27.640
their own. They're human and they have their own&nbsp;
incentives and again, the original, constitutional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:27.640 --> 00:50:36.080
incentives of the organization might be quite&nbsp;
different. This phenomenon is a fact of life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:36.080 --> 00:50:44.560
and for me these kinds of explanations are&nbsp;
much more useful in trying to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:44.560 --> 00:50:49.760
why some of these things either happen or don't.
And to your question: "In as much as Stripe has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:49.760 --> 00:50:57.320
moat, what is it?" Others can judge to what degree&nbsp;
it's actually manifested and rooted in practice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:57.320 --> 00:51:01.800
I think it is, but I'm a biased observer.&nbsp;
I think it would be, that people at Stripe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:01.800 --> 00:51:06.920
really care about solving the problems
that we say we are trying to solve.&nbsp;

00:51:06.920 --> 00:51:14.040
Yeah, the point about the misalignment over&nbsp;
generations or over time is interesting. Do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:14.040 --> 00:51:18.200
you have examples of institutions, which have&nbsp;
for decades or centuries managed to keep their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:18.200 --> 00:51:24.040
original, not only mission statement, but&nbsp;
organizational competence? Because if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:24.040 --> 00:51:28.360
think of tech companies, even the oldest&nbsp;
ones have not been around that long, right?&nbsp;

00:51:28.360 --> 00:51:31.240
And they're some of the biggest tech companies in&nbsp;
the world. And the median age of the corporation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:31.240 --> 00:51:38.680
is famously low. What is a good example here?
Some of the explanations around the effects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:52.980
shareholder capitalism suggest that it influences&nbsp;
the incentives of organizations and their long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:52.980 --> 00:52:00.280
term fates. Those theories have some credibility&nbsp;
and it’s plausible that shareholder capitalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:00.280 --> 00:52:05.320
even attenuates the duration of some of these&nbsp;
organizations. I'm not saying that's definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:05.320 --> 00:52:10.640
true, but I find the idea that it could be,&nbsp;
incredible. It's unclear if that's necessarily bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:10.640 --> 00:52:16.560
if it is true, right? In that, are we on the side&nbsp;
of the humans, aggregate innovation in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:16.560 --> 00:52:24.200
or corporations, or quad legal entities? The&nbsp;
answer isn’t clear to me. It should be the third.&nbsp;

00:52:24.200 --> 00:52:39.640
At the same time, if you look at Europe or places&nbsp;
like Denmark, because of the tax code there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:39.640 --> 00:52:47.920
a lot of organizations are either controlled or&nbsp;
substantially held by non-profit foundations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:47.920 --> 00:52:54.960
For example Novo Nordisk – the GLP company,&nbsp;
Maersk – the shipping company, I believe also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:54.960 --> 00:53:01.000
Lego. A lot of these corporations are controlled&nbsp;
by foundations and usually have a lot of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:01.000 --> 00:53:08.112
stock held by them. In many cases that has the&nbsp;
secondary effect, where they actually embed their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:08.112 --> 00:53:15.960
mission into a legally binding constitution. I'm&nbsp;
not an expert on Novo Nordisk, but I happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:22.520
get a book about it over Thanksgiving and there’s&nbsp;
also a book on the Danish Industrial Foundations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:24.080 --> 00:53:29.440
It's enshrined in their constitution that they&nbsp;
have to make insulin broadly available and really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:29.440 --> 00:53:32.440
cheap or at least that is the case in Scandinavian&nbsp;
countries. So it is allowed to charge market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:32.440 --> 00:53:46.160
prices elsewhere but they're legally obligated to&nbsp;
reinvest profits in R&amp;D. Is that somehow causal in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:46.160 --> 00:53:51.240
the fact that they made one of the most remarkable&nbsp;
pharmacological discoveries of the last 20 years:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:53.120 --> 00:54:06.880
GLP-1 agonists? – Plausibly. These questions of&nbsp;
“Why is it that the median age of organizations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:06.880 --> 00:54:12.000
and corporations is what it is?” are definitely&nbsp;
interesting and I suspect the reason is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:12.520 --> 00:54:18.400
somewhat dependent on the way we've chosen to&nbsp;
organize large corporations in the US today.&nbsp;

00:54:18.400 --> 00:54:22.080
The thing you're mentioning about this firm&nbsp;
seems very similar to the export-led growth&nbsp;

00:54:22.080 --> 00:54:24.800
in Asia.
Totally.&nbsp;

00:54:24.800 --> 00:54:27.640
The idea of tariffs. There's one company&nbsp;
tasked with making the cars, so you better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:27.640 --> 00:54:31.680
make the cars good. You have no competition but&nbsp;
you have to invent the best car in the world.&nbsp;

00:54:31.680 --> 00:54:46.000
Yes, we are all fans of Smith, Ricardo and&nbsp;
even they are less dogmatically attached to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:52.520
free trade than people today interpret them as&nbsp;
being. People like Friedrich List and other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:52.520 --> 00:54:57.640
not quite contemporaries, but quasi-contemporaries&nbsp;
are underrated on a relative basis.&nbsp;

00:54:59.840 --> 00:55:08.640
As much as you believe the kind of sociological,&nbsp;
cultural skill, even vague alignment in the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:08.640 --> 00:55:15.840
interpersonal sense, in as much as you think these&nbsp;
are important and explanatory, then you end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:15.840 --> 00:55:19.800
thinking about some of the things the US raised.
That's really interesting to hear you say that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:19.800 --> 00:55:24.560
because if you think about Stripe's&nbsp;
mission– it's to facilitate global trade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:24.560 --> 00:55:32.400
to make sure that some firm from India can compete&nbsp;
with any firm in Nigeria or whatever. So the room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:32.400 --> 00:55:35.320
for you to have this sort of learning curve&nbsp;
where you're less efficient than the global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:35.320 --> 00:55:41.120
competition should be less, if Stripe exists,&nbsp;
right? Isn't Stripe the anti-List company?&nbsp;

00:55:41.120 --> 00:55:47.120
Well, it depends which version of List. To be&nbsp;
clear, I'm not specifically endorsing these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:48.240 --> 00:55:55.000
tariffs and trade barriers. The history associated&nbsp;
with them is checkered at best. Look, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:55.000 --> 00:56:07.400
possible that if you have a specific sector where&nbsp;
you have clear goals, a credible path to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:07.400 --> 00:56:14.880
achieving some substantial degree of success and&nbsp;
some conjoined propositions, then some degree of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:16.480 --> 00:56:26.400
activist trade policy might be the beneficial&nbsp;
thing to do. I don't think that that describes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:26.400 --> 00:56:34.680
most sectors in most countries at most times.
That's so interesting. I think there's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:34.680 --> 00:56:39.200
interesting thread here in how it relates to&nbsp;
Stripe climate, in that you're subsidizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:39.200 --> 00:56:44.160
learning curves that East Asian countries&nbsp;
did for their own internal companies. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:44.160 --> 00:56:49.640
haven't picked out a specific company that's&nbsp;
going to necessarily be the key to carbon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:49.640 --> 00:56:54.880
sequestration. How do you think about this?
Well, a way to unify the two points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:54.880 --> 00:57:04.440
and I'll speak about Stripe in a second, is&nbsp;
Say's law about demand creating supply. In as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:04.440 --> 00:57:11.040
much as Stripe aggregates more and more global&nbsp;
demand, it seems too self-aggrandizing to call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:11.040 --> 00:57:16.480
it “The theory of Stripe", but some vague&nbsp;
hunch in Stripe is that this aggregation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:16.480 --> 00:57:21.840
of demand can have important expansionary&nbsp;
effects with respect to the ensuing supply.&nbsp;

00:57:21.840 --> 00:57:26.760
Stripe climate is some version of this hypothesis,&nbsp;
applied on a much smaller scale than Stripe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:26.760 --> 00:57:33.920
itself, but still real and maybe important. For&nbsp;
folks who aren't familiar, which I assume is most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:33.920 --> 00:57:40.160
of your audience, the basic idea goes like this;&nbsp;
We observed in 2018, that everyone seems to agree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:40.160 --> 00:57:45.960
that carbon removal will be very important.&nbsp;
Even if we decarbonize the economy on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:49.640 --> 00:57:55.120
most optimistic timeframes, there'll still be&nbsp;
an accumulated stock of carbon, which will be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:55.120 --> 00:58:00.760
problem. It sounded pretty weird, that there&nbsp;
were virtually no carbon removal companies in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:00.760 --> 00:58:06.840
world in 2018. Maybe there were two or three. No&nbsp;
companies had ever purchased from carbon removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:06.840 --> 00:58:11.520
companies, which were really sort of science&nbsp;
projects. So we thought: "Well, somebody's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:11.520 --> 00:58:16.760
got to start and it might be valuable to not&nbsp;
only transfer some dollars, but to confer some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:16.760 --> 00:58:20.280
credibility on this sector." Not that Stripe is&nbsp;
the world's most credible company, but it's better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:20.280 --> 00:58:25.280
than nothing. So we started contracting with&nbsp;
some of these carbon removal companies. That went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:25.280 --> 00:58:29.520
pretty well and they seemed kind of appreciative&nbsp;
of us and so we thought somewhat more about this.&nbsp;

00:58:30.680 --> 00:58:36.760
Then, in 2021, we formed Frontier, which is an&nbsp;
AMC, an advanced market commitment. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:36.760 --> 00:58:45.320
inspired by the first AMC, which was a&nbsp;
pre-commitment to purchase vaccines for developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:45.320 --> 00:58:51.520
world's countries for diseases that were market&nbsp;
failures, where either pharma companies hadn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:51.520 --> 00:58:55.840
pursued the vaccines, or where the profits weren't&nbsp;
sufficient to pay for the program. So we decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:55.840 --> 00:59:03.440
to do this for carbon removal. We raised a billion&nbsp;
dollars. Stripe was the first investor. We're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:03.440 --> 00:59:07.280
actually investing, we're just buying, so we were&nbsp;
the first company to commit. Then we were joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:07.280 --> 00:59:16.080
by Shopify and Alphabet and Meta and JP Morgan&nbsp;
and a bunch of other companies. And now there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:16.080 --> 00:59:24.880
fairly active sector of carbon removal companies.
Frontier has contracted with between 40 and 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:24.880 --> 00:59:30.080
companies, the overwhelming majority of which&nbsp;
didn't exist when we started out with this. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:30.080 --> 00:59:37.400
ran an anonymous survey back at the end of last&nbsp;
year, where we asked them to what degree was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:37.400 --> 00:59:42.520
existence of Frontier causal in their starting&nbsp;
the company in the first place. Again, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:42.520 --> 00:59:49.480
an anonymous survey. I think it was 74% of the&nbsp;
companies that said that Frontier played a causal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:49.480 --> 00:59:57.160
role in their starting the company. So these&nbsp;
inducement effects can be pretty significant.&nbsp;

00:59:57.160 --> 01:00:01.440
Yeah, that's huge. What are other ideas you've&nbsp;
come across, where an AMC would be an effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:01.440 --> 01:00:05.720
instrument of moving forward the tech?
That's a good question. We've been having&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:05.720 --> 01:00:11.680
that discussion internally. It's not that we&nbsp;
plan on doing it ourselves necessarily, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:11.680 --> 01:00:19.440
I'm wondering: "Are there people we should share&nbsp;
our technology with?" Not even technology per se,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:19.440 --> 01:00:29.440
but share our experience and try to help along.&nbsp;
There's still a lot of stuff in the biomedical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:29.440 --> 01:00:42.800
fields. Patents are pretty useful insofar as&nbsp;
they go, but there's a lot of innovation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:42.800 --> 01:00:50.720
seems socially beneficial, that patents&nbsp;
don't provide a way to cover the cost of.&nbsp;

01:00:52.600 --> 01:01:04.000
There was some excitement a few years ago about&nbsp;
mannose, which is a sugar. There was one or maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:04.000 --> 01:01:11.680
a few papers that suggest that maybe tumors will&nbsp;
selectively take up mannose rather than glucose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:11.680 --> 01:01:16.360
but they won't actually metabolize it&nbsp;
properly and they'll just die. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:16.360 --> 01:01:26.080
this could be an effective onco-treatment.
Mannose is like a generic sugar. It's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:26.080 --> 01:01:30.240
understood for more than a century&nbsp;
and, importantly, you couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:30.240 --> 01:01:37.680
patent it. So it's not clear who has the&nbsp;
incentive to even fund the work to test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:37.680 --> 01:01:42.360
whether or not this would actually work in&nbsp;
practice. This is not an endorsement of mannose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:42.360 --> 01:01:47.320
but there are things of this shape, where there's&nbsp;
something that clearly might be very beneficial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:47.320 --> 01:01:51.760
but it's not clear how the economic structure of&nbsp;
the market can make it possible. There are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:51.760 --> 01:01:56.520
a lot of those across the biomedical landscape.
There are still a lot of vaccines that could,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:56.520 --> 01:02:03.680
in principle, exist that don't, like Lyme disease.&nbsp;
There was one vaccine that was withdrawn from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:03.680 --> 01:02:09.360
the market over safety concerns, that I think&nbsp;
were misplaced, but there's still no vaccine.&nbsp;

01:02:09.360 --> 01:02:12.880
It's not even that well understood,&nbsp;
right? People have chronic Lyme&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:12.880 --> 01:02:20.280
disease. We don't know if it's legit or not.
Exactly. But it's a good question. Maybe some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:20.280 --> 01:02:25.160
of your listeners will have ideas for&nbsp;
fields, where we sorely need an AMC.&nbsp;

01:02:26.720 --> 01:02:34.120
I want to go back to Stripe for a second. So&nbsp;
you're famously appreciative of craft and beauty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:34.120 --> 01:02:36.960
but you also appreciate the&nbsp;
power of scale and growth.&nbsp;

01:02:37.520 --> 01:02:42.160
And speed.
Oh, interesting. Is there a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:42.160 --> 01:02:47.240
type of craft that is just not amenable to speed,&nbsp;
growth, scale? If you think of a Japanese chef,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:47.240 --> 01:02:51.280
he's been learning to cook rice for a&nbsp;
decade, and then he can move on to sushi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:52.400 --> 01:03:00.480
Is that just not competitive in the modern world?
Craft, scale, and speed. I don't know if they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:00.480 --> 01:03:04.440
strictly necessarily intentioned in every case,&nbsp;
but they're definitely frequently intentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:04.440 --> 01:03:14.520
so yes is the short answer to that. At the same&nbsp;
time, a lot of the most successful companies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:14.520 --> 01:03:24.600
those that are distinguished by the extent&nbsp;
to which they exhibit appreciation for and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:24.600 --> 01:03:30.600
skill in realizing craft and beauty. LVMH is&nbsp;
one of the largest companies in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:30.600 --> 01:03:36.040
and that's literally their business. Tesla is&nbsp;
pretty good at this. They're good at many things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:36.600 --> 01:03:47.160
including this. Obviously, there's Apple. TSMC&nbsp;
is not the Japanese sushi chef you mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:47.160 --> 01:03:58.080
but it's the TSMC chip sushi chef in Taiwan. They&nbsp;
have so much tacit knowledge and difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:58.080 --> 01:04:10.800
transfer skills. It might be the case that craft&nbsp;
and the pursuit of it is as important as it's ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:10.800 --> 01:04:22.280
been. Certainly, as Stripe has gotten larger,&nbsp;
we have come to greater conviction in this.&nbsp;

01:04:24.960 --> 01:04:30.240
Part of what's interesting about these&nbsp;
aesthetic qualities is they're generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:30.240 --> 01:04:33.040
speaking unquantifiable. I don't know&nbsp;
if they're intrinsically unquantifiable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:33.040 --> 01:04:38.960
maybe you could train a model to do so, but today,&nbsp;
they are broadly speaking unquantifiable. And yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:42.640 --> 01:04:49.400
they influence people in significant ways. People&nbsp;
very demonstrably care about aesthetics. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:49.400 --> 01:04:53.880
they're a company, they care about the aesthetic&nbsp;
characteristics of the products that they produce.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:53.880 --> 01:05:02.680
On an intuitive level, people know that that's&nbsp;
true. But it's difficult to manage that at an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:02.680 --> 01:05:07.080
organizational level, where there isn't a P&amp;L&nbsp;
associated with it, and if you're screwing it up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:07.080 --> 01:05:16.400
you don't see a neat time series decline.
Over the 14 years of Stripe, we have, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:16.400 --> 01:05:19.960
exactly through trial and error, but by studying&nbsp;
cases where things worked well and less well at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:19.960 --> 01:05:29.120
Stripe, what customers responded well to, and so&nbsp;
on, understood, that even in a domain like ours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:29.120 --> 01:05:39.320
where we are selling primarily to businesses, that&nbsp;
is something that's truly important. Getting back&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:39.320 --> 01:05:48.840
to what we were discussing previously, in as much&nbsp;
as the sociology and "cultural" explanations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:48.840 --> 01:05:55.960
defensibility are real, the best people consider&nbsp;
themselves crafts people in their domain and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:55.960 --> 01:06:05.160
really, above almost all else, want to work with&nbsp;
the best other people. It may almost be true, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:05.160 --> 01:06:11.520
even if from a customer-facing standpoint, craft&nbsp;
was not valued by the market, you might still want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:11.520 --> 01:06:16.680
to build an organization that indexes very heavily&nbsp;
on this, because you just want the best people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:16.680 --> 01:06:21.680
for other reasons. Now, as it happens, I think&nbsp;
customers do, in fact, value it. The evidence is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:21.680 --> 01:06:30.400
broadly consistent with that. It's very hard to&nbsp;
assemble groups of the best people, if you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:30.400 --> 01:06:36.320
take the practice of the work super seriously.
What kind of beauty or craft or simplicity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:36.320 --> 01:06:39.400
is more important— interface or&nbsp;
implementation?There's famously&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:39.400 --> 01:06:44.400
that essay that Unix is successful because the&nbsp;
implementation is simple and not the interface.&nbsp;

01:06:44.400 --> 01:06:51.640
The interface is kind of simple, but&nbsp;
there's a lot of edge cases that I guess&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:51.640 --> 01:06:56.680
Unix doesn't handle for you.
But Stripe does, right?&nbsp;

01:06:57.280 --> 01:07:02.320
Presumably, it depends what you're building. For&nbsp;
TikTok, it's more important that their interface&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:02.320 --> 01:07:06.200
is simple. Even if their implementation is a&nbsp;
mess, that's probably OK. Not saying it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:06.200 --> 01:07:15.960
I have no idea. Whereas for Stripe, people are,&nbsp;
on some level, purchasing our architecture or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:15.960 --> 01:07:20.800
purchasing their ability to do certain things&nbsp;
rather than some different set of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:21.320 --> 01:07:28.120
because of what our architecture makes easy&nbsp;
and possible. If by interface you mean the GUI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:30.520 --> 01:07:35.120
then maybe we can draw some separation there. But&nbsp;
we don't really draw that distinction. We think of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:35.120 --> 01:07:43.800
the interface to Stripe as being the architecture.
No one else seems to agree with me, but I often&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:43.800 --> 01:07:54.600
think of Stripe as similar to Mathematica, where&nbsp;
we're selling a self-contained universe to model&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:54.600 --> 01:08:05.840
whatever it is of interest to you. We're providing&nbsp;
some primitives, interfaces and tools and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:05.840 --> 01:08:12.360
forth, to enable your modeling. But fundamentally,&nbsp;
we're helping you do something on your own terms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:13.400 --> 01:08:18.200
In that sense, I don't think the architecture&nbsp;
and the interface are necessarily that separable.&nbsp;

01:08:18.200 --> 01:08:24.480
That's a really interesting analogy. Although, if&nbsp;
you think of Mathematica, the entry that that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:24.480 --> 01:08:30.920
giving you to, is just the platonic objects&nbsp;
of math, whereas for you guys, the entry is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:30.920 --> 01:08:42.640
Visa error codes. The end object is not platonic.
That's true. So yes, the analogy falls down in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:42.640 --> 01:08:51.640
few respects. But look, the idea of a transaction&nbsp;
is pretty fundamental and is roughly as old as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:52.160 --> 01:09:01.280
the quadratic equation. I guess the transaction's&nbsp;
older. And Mathematica now supports all kinds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:01.280 --> 01:09:07.680
crazy, arcane stuff, to a very impressive extent.&nbsp;
If you go through the more obscure packages in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:07.680 --> 01:09:13.360
Mathematica, you can definitely find things that&nbsp;
are much less broadly employed and understood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:13.360 --> 01:09:18.520
even less than Visa error codes. But yes, these&nbsp;
are not the same, It's just that I find it to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:19.240 --> 01:09:23.440
an interesting source of intuition. What Wolfram&nbsp;
has done with Mathematica is pretty amazing.&nbsp;

01:09:24.040 --> 01:09:28.680
Another way, in which I'm curious how you think&nbsp;
about this, is: one way in which Mathematica&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:28.680 --> 01:09:32.240
maybe differs is, if they had to make a change&nbsp;
in Mathematica — "Big deal, somebody has to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:32.240 --> 01:09:42.320
new syntax". If you make a change — billions of&nbsp;
dollars of transactions don't happen. How does&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:42.320 --> 01:09:46.040
that change the way you think about the initial
architecture and the stakes?&nbsp;

01:09:46.840 --> 01:09:54.680
It's a good question. First a point on beauty with&nbsp;
respect to architecture and then I'll answer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:54.680 --> 01:10:01.400
one. Just as a side note, it's interesting that&nbsp;
API design in general doesn't get more study as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:01.400 --> 01:10:09.160
a discipline and as a practice. It plays or can&nbsp;
play a significant role in the fate of platforms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:10.520 --> 01:10:17.360
Not saying it is always the determinative thing.&nbsp;
But if you get it right, there can be compounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:17.360 --> 01:10:25.280
positive benefits and the converse. It's really&nbsp;
striking that, say with mobile app development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:25.280 --> 01:10:32.520
which was one of the most dynamic ecosystems of&nbsp;
the past 10 or 15 years, so many of the objects&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:32.520 --> 01:10:39.840
and the classes, say in iOS development, are&nbsp;
prefixed with NS. Less so now with Swift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:39.840 --> 01:10:46.440
but for much of the iPhone's history. The NS&nbsp;
refers to Next app, back from Next in the 90s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:47.200 --> 01:10:55.000
When you get API design and architecture right,&nbsp;
it can be so enduring over literally multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:55.000 --> 01:11:04.840
decades, even in the face of what are otherwise&nbsp;
frenzied evolutions in everything around it.&nbsp;

01:11:04.840 --> 01:11:11.800
Unix is another example of this. Yes, Unix&nbsp;
has tons of shortcomings, but the architecture&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:11.800 --> 01:11:21.280
has worked now for more than half a century.
We're all trying to impress upon people at Stripe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:21.280 --> 01:11:28.920
the importance of multi-decadal abstractions.&nbsp;
People sometimes respond to that thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:28.920 --> 01:11:39.320
that that's some insanely lofty, implausibly&nbsp;
ambitious hyperbola, but no, that's what happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:40.040 --> 01:11:46.720
when you get this stuff right.
In fact, if you get it right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:49.840 --> 01:11:55.000
people building on your platform can reap&nbsp;
incredible benefits for a very long time.&nbsp;

01:11:55.000 --> 01:11:59.660
To the Mathematica point, I know they take&nbsp;
backwards compatibility really seriously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:59.660 --> 01:12:08.400
to the point where you can run programs written 20&nbsp;
years ago, unchanged, in today's Mathematica. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:08.400 --> 01:12:14.920
really raises the stakes in API design for sort of&nbsp;
obvious reasons. We have that problem ourselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:14.920 --> 01:12:19.640
where, when we think about introducing something&nbsp;
new, it's not just: "Does this exigently address&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:19.640 --> 01:12:25.200
the particular need that's motivating it today?"&nbsp;
— but: "Do we think we can stand behind this in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:25.200 --> 01:12:33.320
2044? How do we think the world might evolve&nbsp;
around us, such that it all remains coherent?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:33.320 --> 01:12:37.600
We certainly don't always get that right, but&nbsp;
that's, on some level, what we're trying to do.&nbsp;

01:12:37.600 --> 01:12:42.960
Is Visa an example of this? One might even say,&nbsp;
that one of the downsides of being able to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:42.960 --> 01:12:47.160
implementation for many decades in the future&nbsp;
is, even if it's self-sustainable and you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:47.160 --> 01:12:54.920
this ecosystem in equilibrium set around it, if&nbsp;
you can't modify it because of people's local&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:54.920 --> 01:12:58.320
incentives, you get stuck in this equilibrium&nbsp;
that's worse than it could be otherwise.&nbsp;

01:12:58.320 --> 01:13:07.880
I see. The card networks generally, Visa and&nbsp;
MasterCard, are pretty good at equilibrium.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:10.240 --> 01:13:15.320
It's easy to judge today with the world as&nbsp;
it exists in 2024, but you have to look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:15.320 --> 01:13:19.840
the world as it was when they started out and the&nbsp;
particular problems that they were solving. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:19.840 --> 01:13:26.400
you compare the financial landscape in the US or&nbsp;
in the Western world to those in other places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:26.400 --> 01:13:31.880
it's certainly not clear to me that the&nbsp;
US has gotten a bad hand, so to speak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:31.880 --> 01:13:38.680
or is somehow stuck in any meaningful way.
The card networks do a couple of things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:40.520 --> 01:13:49.480
Originally, they were designed to replace&nbsp;
store credit. It was a credit card originally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:49.480 --> 01:13:56.200
not a debit card, right? That was important. The&nbsp;
availability of structured consumer credit is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:56.200 --> 01:14:03.440
pretty big deal and is beneficial, especially&nbsp;
for lower income people. Then, with the advent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:04.000 --> 01:14:11.960
of jet travel, mass market tourism and so forth,&nbsp;
they helped to supplant travelers checks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:13.240 --> 01:14:19.000
various worse alternatives, like carrying cash&nbsp;
around in your bag. Then, with the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:19.000 --> 01:14:25.680
they were substantially involved in enabling&nbsp;
online transactions. The fact that they got&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:25.680 --> 01:14:32.400
the architecture so right, that so many different&nbsp;
use cases were able to be addressed by their core&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:32.400 --> 01:14:40.120
design is really impressive. The guy who designed&nbsp;
all this,Dee Hock, was a remarkable person.&nbsp;

01:14:40.680 --> 01:14:51.200
People complain about interchange. Lest I sound&nbsp;
like a defender of the card ecosystem. You could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:51.200 --> 01:14:55.840
look at it multiple ways, but many people would&nbsp;
consider Stripe to be on the wrong side of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:55.840 --> 01:15:02.320
interchange cost equation, in the sense that&nbsp;
we're giving away the interchange revenue to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:02.320 --> 01:15:09.000
other companies. So I don't think I'm structurally&nbsp;
biased in favor of interchange, and yet, I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:09.000 --> 01:15:17.280
say it's pretty interesting what interchange made&nbsp;
possible. It is a distribution incentive fee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:17.280 --> 01:15:24.960
where you're paying other entities for recruiting&nbsp;
customers, convincing them to get a card, getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:24.960 --> 01:15:29.680
them to maintain the card and to pay it off at the&nbsp;
end of the month etc. So you're paying for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:29.680 --> 01:15:32.560
just the pure distribution. There's a person&nbsp;
at the end of the flight telling you: "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:32.560 --> 01:15:37.280
sign up for the United Credit Card!"&nbsp;
That's what interchange is paying for.&nbsp;

01:15:37.280 --> 01:15:41.840
That guy annoys me.
We'll get to the counterfactuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:41.840 --> 01:15:48.040
in a second. So there's paying for the actual&nbsp;
credit issuance itself and then there's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:48.040 --> 01:15:53.040
customer support and all the ancillary things&nbsp;
around the dispute handling and so forth.&nbsp;

01:15:54.560 --> 01:15:57.640
It is interesting to look at the cases&nbsp;
where, for whatever contingent reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:58.200 --> 01:16:05.120
the card networks didn't rise. Germany is one of&nbsp;
the classic ones. From our vantage point, dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:05.120 --> 01:16:10.040
with the online economy in Germany as compared&nbsp;
to the US is so much worse. If Stripe could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:10.040 --> 01:16:15.120
push a button and have really broadly adopted&nbsp;
cards in Germany à la the US, we would push the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:15.120 --> 01:16:25.600
hell out of that button. You can look at China,&nbsp;
which on the one hand does have Alipay, WePay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:26.200 --> 01:16:32.240
WeChat payments, that are really ubiquitous — in&nbsp;
that sense, they're very digitally enabled from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:32.240 --> 01:16:36.680
a transactional standpoint. On the other hand,&nbsp;
those products tend not to be as sophisticated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:36.680 --> 01:16:44.840
with consumer credit. So yes, the transaction fees&nbsp;
for transferring your money — that's super cheap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:44.840 --> 01:16:47.920
but you need to look at it on a&nbsp;
fully loaded basis, where: "OK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:47.920 --> 01:16:51.240
but what about the cost of actually getting the&nbsp;
credit to make the purchase in the first place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:51.240 --> 01:16:56.280
as a credit card would enable?" And as you look&nbsp;
at these other counterfactuals in other places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:56.280 --> 01:17:03.880
one feels gratitude for what it is, that Dee Hock&nbsp;
and Visa and MasterCard and the card networks made&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:03.880 --> 01:17:14.760
possible. I'm not saying they're perfect, that one&nbsp;
can't make critiques, but I'm most interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:14.760 --> 01:17:19.640
critiques from people who've really studied the&nbsp;
ecosystems of other countries, because it's easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:19.640 --> 01:17:28.520
to underestimate what we got in their invention.
Maybe there's a Chesterton’s fence kind of thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:28.520 --> 01:17:33.640
going on here. If you had to design payments&nbsp;
from first principles now, does it make sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:33.640 --> 01:17:38.280
that all these things you've mentioned:&nbsp;
taking on credit risk, the chance of fraud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:38.280 --> 01:17:43.600
dispute adjudication, should that cost 2%&nbsp;
or 3% of each transaction that happens in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:43.600 --> 01:17:49.000
the economy? What would payments look like if&nbsp;
you had to design that from first principles?&nbsp;

01:17:49.000 --> 01:17:54.800
We're seeing a live version of this&nbsp;
experiment play out for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:54.800 --> 01:17:59.600
time in many years in a number of countries&nbsp;
today, where central banks are becoming more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:59.600 --> 01:18:10.560
active in designing national payment schemes.
PIX in Brazil launched in late 2020. I'm sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:10.560 --> 01:18:17.080
you've heard of UPI. UPI was the instigator in&nbsp;
this process. It's the central bank payment system&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:17.080 --> 01:18:22.080
in India. And it was tied up with AadHaar and&nbsp;
their national identity system and so on. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:22.080 --> 01:18:25.760
inspired a lot of central bankers in other&nbsp;
countries to go and build their own UPIs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:25.760 --> 01:18:31.680
So PICS in Brazil launched in 2020 and now a&nbsp;
significant majority of all Brazilian adults&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:31.680 --> 01:18:37.400
are weekly active users of PICS. Again, even&nbsp;
though it launched in 2020. So it just had this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:37.400 --> 01:18:46.760
incredibly rapid adoption curve. You have Swish&nbsp;
in Sweden, There are examples across East Asia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:46.760 --> 01:18:52.400
Japan, Thailand, Switzerland. Central bank&nbsp;
after central bank is deciding: "Hey, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:52.400 --> 01:18:58.240
should have our version of this." This is a kind&nbsp;
of reinvention of the payment system from scratch.&nbsp;

01:18:59.560 --> 01:19:06.280
For some weird reasons hard to understand,&nbsp;
once you layer in the customer support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:06.280 --> 01:19:14.480
consumer protection, fraud prevention,&nbsp;
anti-money laundering controls and the credit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:14.480 --> 01:19:22.240
things seem to asymptote at around 2% or 3%.&nbsp;
It's important to also note that beyond just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:22.240 --> 01:19:28.440
covering the costs, much of it ends up&nbsp;
getting remitted to consumers in the form&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:28.440 --> 01:19:37.480
of rewards, not in every country, but in many&nbsp;
countries. If you look at the public reports&nbsp;

01:19:37.480 --> 01:19:44.880
from various banks in the US, their interchange&nbsp;
revenue, where they're getting these delicious&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:44.880 --> 01:19:48.440
fees on every transaction, as you put it, a&nbsp;
lot of that is going straight back out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:48.440 --> 01:19:53.480
door to the consumers. So it's not clear how&nbsp;
exactly one should think about economics. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:53.480 --> 01:19:56.080
it's going back to the consumer, should&nbsp;
you include that as a transaction tax&nbsp;

01:19:56.080 --> 01:20:03.520
or is it just like a weird circular relationship?&nbsp;
I've not seen any evidence to suggest that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:04.200 --> 01:20:11.680
2% or thereabouts is massively inefficient in the&nbsp;
scheme of things. I'm not saying it's the optimal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:11.680 --> 01:20:18.320
level— maybe 1% would be better, but within some&nbsp;
range of 1% to 3%, it's probably reasonable.&nbsp;

01:20:21.000 --> 01:20:35.400
As we think about these ad valorem fees and&nbsp;
figures, the place where there's even more change&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:35.400 --> 01:20:41.280
at the moment that we find ourselves thinking more&nbsp;
about is the changing structure of global tax.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:46.760 --> 01:20:54.120
There's been a reasonable amount of innovation in&nbsp;
the tax domain over the last century: income taxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:54.120 --> 01:21:01.520
got pretty high, then we added value taxes, and so&nbsp;
on. The new thing, at least in the online context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:01.520 --> 01:21:12.000
is jurisdictions remitting or imposing&nbsp;
sales taxes on businesses that don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:12.000 --> 01:21:19.400
any "locus" in the jurisdiction in question.
So if you're a podcaster in the Bay Area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:20.280 --> 01:21:29.760
hypothetically "Dwarkesh merch store" will have&nbsp;
to pay the town of Uppsala in Sweden, which will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:29.760 --> 01:21:36.680
have a special tax on baseball caps. And you will&nbsp;
need to know about that particular tax on baseball&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:36.680 --> 01:21:40.880
caps. And for any baseball caps that you are&nbsp;
selling to the Uppsalians, you'll have to collect&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:40.880 --> 01:21:48.600
that amount from the buyer, report to Uppsala, and&nbsp;
then eventually figure out how you're going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:48.600 --> 01:21:52.640
that money to Uppsala. Obviously, it's this&nbsp;
combinatorial problem of buyer jurisdictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:53.720 --> 01:22:00.720
and product types, and then all the different&nbsp;
jurisdictions that you have to remit the money to.&nbsp;

01:22:01.400 --> 01:22:08.960
As to those amounts, we're not talking three&nbsp;
basis points — the taxes in question are often&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:08.960 --> 01:22:18.600
5% or 10%, so it's not trivial. As I think about&nbsp;
the funds flows on the internet and how all that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:18.600 --> 01:22:23.120
evolving and unfolding, I think changes in&nbsp;
tax law are actually a much bigger deal than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:23.120 --> 01:22:26.600
anything about the transactional economics.
By the way, it's not the Dwarkesh podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:26.600 --> 01:22:32.320
it's Lunar Society Podcast LLC registered&nbsp;
on Stripe Atlas. Any merchandise I sell in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:32.320 --> 01:22:36.400
the future, Stripe will take care of that.
Yes, OK. If anyone has Stripe complaints.&nbsp;

01:22:36.400 --> 01:22:40.240
No, it's great, It has been super useful,&nbsp;
honestly. It would have been much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:40.240 --> 01:22:44.200
difficult to get business operations going.
Sorry, I know you're supposed to be interviewing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:44.200 --> 01:22:51.880
me, but did Stripe play any, even on the margins,&nbsp;
counterfactual role in you charging for anything?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:51.880 --> 01:22:54.800
This is the thing we're always interested in. When&nbsp;
we talk about growing the GDP of the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:54.800 --> 01:22:59.120
it's not like: "Get the existing GDP onto our&nbsp;
rails", — it's sort of: "Where on the margin&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:59.800 --> 01:23:04.760
can we cause there to be economic activity&nbsp;
that isn't already occurring?" So yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:05.880 --> 01:23:13.200
you did, in fact, start the podcast before&nbsp;
incorporating, but were we causal in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:13.200 --> 01:23:21.960
fashion in the merch or anything of that nature?
To the extent that Substack would not be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:21.960 --> 01:23:26.800
convenient place to get payments from to begin&nbsp;
with, that's definitely a thing. And also...&nbsp;

01:23:27.440 --> 01:23:30.360
You wouldn't charge for the newsletter&nbsp;
if Substack hadn't made it super easy?&nbsp;

01:23:30.360 --> 01:23:35.160
Yeah. And also, if I do an ad, I wouldn't&nbsp;
even know how to begin with getting the money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:35.160 --> 01:23:39.960
if I didn't already have an LLC through&nbsp;
Stripe with the dissociative bank account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:39.960 --> 01:23:45.040
that I'm going to get the money through. So yeah,&nbsp;
probably counterfactually responsible for a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:45.040 --> 01:23:46.840
of the monetization.
That's cool.&nbsp;

01:23:49.000 --> 01:23:55.800
Appreciate it. What are some unexpected&nbsp;
complements to payment processing you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:55.800 --> 01:24:04.600
see in the future? All this stuff: Atlas,&nbsp;
identity fraud, detection — in retrospect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:04.600 --> 01:24:08.440
it might not have been obvious back then there&nbsp;
was a good complement, but now it does seem that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:08.440 --> 01:24:20.320
way. What would be like this in five, 10 years?
Honestly, our problem ends up being that more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:20.320 --> 01:24:27.880
things then we could possibly pursue, look like&nbsp;
complements. In that every business almost by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:27.880 --> 01:24:35.480
definition has revenue we obviously want to help&nbsp;
them generate, accept, manage and orchestrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:35.480 --> 01:24:41.200
everything pertaining to that revenue. But&nbsp;
once you're in that flow and you go through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:41.200 --> 01:24:49.680
the steps of running a business, a lot else looks&nbsp;
relevant and somehow connects quite directly.&nbsp;

01:24:54.720 --> 01:25:02.320
When Stripe started out, it definitely wasn't&nbsp;
cool. It was the opposite, it was just a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:02.320 --> 01:25:09.000
of us and we thought that we could make this&nbsp;
superior payments API. For the vast majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:09.000 --> 01:25:17.960
of its history, Stripe has attracted people&nbsp;
who are drawn to unglamorous infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:17.960 --> 01:25:27.400
challenges and problems. We're not a company&nbsp;
that specializes in making beautiful cars— we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:27.400 --> 01:25:36.560
make roads. I bring all of that up, because&nbsp;
it's relevant to this complement question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:36.560 --> 01:25:43.440
where in our discussions internally, a lot of&nbsp;
it, probably the significant majority of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:43.440 --> 01:25:49.840
is still about: "OK, where are there practical&nbsp;
shortcomings and limitations in even our core&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:49.840 --> 01:25:56.520
bread and butter?" Payment processing might be a&nbsp;
slightly too limited term to use for us. It's more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:56.520 --> 01:26:05.000
about global programmable money orchestration,&nbsp;
which, yes, is consumer to business payments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:05.000 --> 01:26:07.920
the sort that we were just discussing in, say,&nbsp;
the context of your Substack. But it's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:07.920 --> 01:26:15.040
business to business payments, payments where&nbsp;
there's credit or lending involved. It's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:15.040 --> 01:26:21.720
how you hold money, how you convert money between&nbsp;
different currencies. It's how you represent money&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:21.720 --> 01:26:27.880
that's held by different legal entities and how&nbsp;
we make it possible for even individuals or small&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:27.880 --> 01:26:36.320
businesses to act as micro multinationals.
But those problems that we just skimmed over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:37.080 --> 01:26:43.200
even though they all directly pertain to the&nbsp;
movement of money, they're not small. If we could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:43.200 --> 01:26:53.080
just solve those really effectively, then Stripe&nbsp;
will be a very consequential organization and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:53.080 --> 01:26:59.560
force in the world. The counterfactual importance&nbsp;
of building some of this stuff, as we go to newer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:59.560 --> 01:27:05.360
markets that are, on a relative basis, more poorly&nbsp;
served, is increasing rather than shrinking. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:05.360 --> 01:27:08.920
the US, there were payments companies before&nbsp;
Stripe and maybe, if Stripe had never done its&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:08.920 --> 01:27:14.400
thing, eventually you'd have found some way to&nbsp;
monetize a newsletter or something like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:14.400 --> 01:27:27.160
But if you're in Albania, the set of options&nbsp;
available to you is far more restricted. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:27.160 --> 01:27:35.360
marginal impact as we expand globally increases&nbsp;
quite a bit. Even though we are interested in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:35.360 --> 01:27:43.280
do pursue some of these direct adjacencies&nbsp;
today, the core problem of global money&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:43.280 --> 01:27:51.680
orchestration remains really big and unsolved.
Does that look like being a better interface&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:51.680 --> 01:27:56.400
for all these complexities and glossing them over&nbsp;
under the seven lines of code? Or does that look&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:56.400 --> 01:28:02.200
like replacing the rails and the infrastructure&nbsp;
to make all this more efficient and effective?&nbsp;

01:28:02.200 --> 01:28:09.040
The former. It's not that useful to build&nbsp;
financial ecosystems, that are self-contained.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:09.680 --> 01:28:17.920
A financial island is not that helpful. It's&nbsp;
much more valuable to build a financial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:17.920 --> 01:28:28.880
this is mixing metaphors, but a financial "air&nbsp;
network". We would much prefer that Stripe plugged&nbsp;

01:28:28.880 --> 01:28:39.080
into every existing system, rail, domestic&nbsp;
organization, rather than that we tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:39.080 --> 01:28:42.320
come along and supplant them. And this has&nbsp;
been Stripe's strategy very deliberately&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:42.320 --> 01:28:47.280
from the beginning, where there were lots&nbsp;
of companies, when Stripe started out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:47.280 --> 01:28:52.440
that were trying to do their own thing and&nbsp;
go their own way, whereas our belief was:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:52.440 --> 01:28:58.520
you get these classic Metcalfe's law stuff —&nbsp;
by enhancing the capabilities of an existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:58.520 --> 01:29:05.000
ecosystem, you create quite a bit more value.
OK, let's go back to Stripe. Is Stripe a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:05.000 --> 01:29:08.520
writing culture for the benefit&nbsp;
of the writer or the reader?&nbsp;

01:29:08.520 --> 01:29:12.240
It can be both.
But which one is the more so?&nbsp;

01:29:12.240 --> 01:29:18.040
There are really considerable benefits on both&nbsp;
sides, because for the reader, it's not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:18.040 --> 01:29:21.520
that it's maybe more efficient to communicate&nbsp;
stuff through text, though in many cases it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:21.520 --> 01:29:28.080
but also there's intertemporal benefit, where&nbsp;
future readers can try to understand the through&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:28.080 --> 01:29:34.240
line and the thought process that led us to&nbsp;
this point. And that's very considerable.&nbsp;

01:29:34.240 --> 01:29:40.560
But it's also true that I and lots of people&nbsp;
write things in order to organize one's own&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:40.560 --> 01:29:48.120
thoughts. If that ability was taken away from&nbsp;
me, I'd be meaningfully less effective. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:48.120 --> 01:29:58.880
exactly those bounce out is hard to say.
They're not actually separable. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:58.880 --> 01:30:05.000
my answer. Literate cultures are just a&nbsp;
different thing. I don't mean literate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:05.000 --> 01:30:11.680
in some kind of faux intellectual way,&nbsp;
textual cultures is a better term here.&nbsp;

01:30:16.880 --> 01:30:27.600
Bruno Latour spoke about how he thinks&nbsp;
the printing revolution, like Gutenberg's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:27.600 --> 01:30:35.400
partially caused the scientific revolution&nbsp;
by making knowledge more rigid. Before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:35.400 --> 01:30:39.440
if some observation didn't match some claim,&nbsp;
you could always shrug and be like: "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:39.440 --> 01:30:44.000
the person who transcribed that thing made&nbsp;
a mistake." So by making things more rigid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:44.000 --> 01:30:51.840
it's easier to break them. Then you can notice&nbsp;
discrepancies between the theory and the reality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:52.400 --> 01:30:56.440
There's some version of that organizationally,&nbsp;
and I'm not drawing a precise parallel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:56.440 --> 01:31:03.680
but there are analogous dynamics, where the&nbsp;
nature of oral cultures and textual cultures are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:03.680 --> 01:31:14.120
just quite different. The kinds of collaboration&nbsp;
that are possible, kinds of consistency, that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:14.120 --> 01:31:25.280
be achieved, are just fundamentally different.&nbsp;
Is the front or rear wheel of the bicycle more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:25.280 --> 01:31:31.400
valuable? Theoretically, you can be a unicycle,&nbsp;
but as a practical matter, you do just need both.&nbsp;

01:31:31.960 --> 01:31:36.200
I know I said no more AI questions, but on this&nbsp;
particular point, it seems very legitimate to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:36.200 --> 01:31:42.400
that you might expect firms, that have a lot&nbsp;
of writing, to be the first to experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:42.400 --> 01:31:45.440
the productivity gains of AI, because in other&nbsp;
cases there's all this context that the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:45.440 --> 01:31:49.320
doesn't have readily available. I don't&nbsp;
know if that's something you anticipate.&nbsp;

01:31:49.320 --> 01:31:53.480
That's probably true. Yeah, I don't&nbsp;
know. If the model is really good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:53.480 --> 01:31:58.360
maybe it's able to pick stuff up quickly.&nbsp;
Most organizations are not recording all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:58.360 --> 01:32:03.960
of their meetings for a variety of reasons, and&nbsp;
if they're not, then there is this question of:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:03.960 --> 01:32:08.680
"What is the corpus? How do you get up to&nbsp;
speed?" So yeah, my guess is that will be true.&nbsp;

01:32:08.680 --> 01:32:16.440
Tell me about the internal LLM you built.
Oh, we didn't build an internal LLM, we built&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:16.440 --> 01:32:25.120
an internal LLM tool for making it very easy for&nbsp;
people to integrate LLMs into production services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:25.120 --> 01:32:33.920
but also into their regular workflows as humans.&nbsp;
We added the ability to work directly with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:33.920 --> 01:32:41.880
LLM, as a standard chat agent, as lots of people&nbsp;
did, but then also to integrate that with some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:41.880 --> 01:32:50.200
our tools for querying and accessing data, most&nbsp;
interestingly, we added sharing prompts across&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:50.200 --> 01:32:54.520
different people, so that somebody might&nbsp;
discover these prompts. One of my favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:54.520 --> 01:33:04.280
examples is: somebody put together a prompt for&nbsp;
optimizing SQL queries. It doesn't always work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:04.280 --> 01:33:11.240
but sometimes it does. It's very cheap to ask us:&nbsp;
"Got any ideas for optimizing the SQL query?" And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:11.240 --> 01:33:20.640
sometimes it will come up with some good stuff.&nbsp;
So the collaborative abilities there have proven&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:21.600 --> 01:33:27.320
surprisingly high return. And then having, lots&nbsp;
of organizations have this — we're not claiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:27.320 --> 01:33:36.760
that it's very novel — but having a central&nbsp;
bus through which to route all access to these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:36.760 --> 01:33:41.440
LLMs, in such way, that we can experiment&nbsp;
with different models and have some degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:41.440 --> 01:33:51.760
of observability into the respective performance&nbsp;
trends and the usage of different cases. We have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:51.760 --> 01:33:58.240
found building a fairly significant amount of&nbsp;
production infrastructure around LLMs to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:58.240 --> 01:34:05.000
valuable. And now, given the proliferation of LLMs&nbsp;
themselves, with all of the obvious contenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:05.000 --> 01:34:08.480
this is proving quite valuable, because we're&nbsp;
able to try to figure out for different use&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:08.480 --> 01:34:13.840
cases which models: self-optimized or&nbsp;
who knows which, are most effective.&nbsp;

01:34:15.240 --> 01:34:21.560
I don't know what the total number of invocations&nbsp;
is, but I think we're making millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:21.560 --> 01:34:27.560
invocations per day now. There are dozens of&nbsp;
dozens of actual production use cases across&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:27.560 --> 01:34:37.320
Stripe. The financial services ecosystem is, in&nbsp;
some way, a giant analog to digital exercise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:37.320 --> 01:34:50.640
because humans, intentions, identities are analog&nbsp;
— all these things have some degree of uncertainty&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:50.640 --> 01:34:58.000
around them and some noise. But then transactions&nbsp;
are digital, right? And we often find in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:58.000 --> 01:35:03.720
analog to digital conversions, that LLMs can&nbsp;
be a surprisingly interesting augmenting tool.&nbsp;

01:35:04.440 --> 01:35:12.800
On that point about the flexibility and the&nbsp;
edge cases in the way humans interact with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:12.800 --> 01:35:20.000
these systems: in some sense, Stripe is a really&nbsp;
high stakes bug bounty program, right? If somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:20.000 --> 01:35:30.800
hacks it, if there's reliability issues, not just&nbsp;
because of a hack, but because you deployed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:30.800 --> 01:35:32.160
wrong way, not only the financial services—&nbsp;
obviously, money's in play— but a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:32.160 --> 01:35:39.040
percentage of rural GDP would grind to a halt, at&nbsp;
least while it's down. How do you deal with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:39.040 --> 01:35:45.240
kind of responsibility? How do you keep the uptime&nbsp;
and keep the reliability while deploying fast?&nbsp;

01:35:45.240 --> 01:35:52.160
This is one of the things we've spent the most&nbsp;
time on. Back to this point about wanting to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:52.160 --> 01:35:58.960
the place with the best people and the value of&nbsp;
focusing on craft, so that you can have the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:58.960 --> 01:36:10.560
people. In the context of software development,&nbsp;
two things developers hate: slow development&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:10.560 --> 01:36:17.320
cycles: it'll ship in the next release in a month&nbsp;
and that kind of thinking. Developers also hate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:17.320 --> 01:36:26.720
being paged at 2 AM for incidents. So, given&nbsp;
the criticality of the businesses that we serve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:27.680 --> 01:36:36.920
which is, in rough terms, 1% of the global economy&nbsp;
— it's not totally clear how to measure this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:36.920 --> 01:36:46.520
because GDP is defined as final goods and Stripe&nbsp;
is not only selling final goods, so, in theory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:46.520 --> 01:36:50.120
there could be a bit of double counting. But&nbsp;
Stripe is mostly selling final goods. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:50.120 --> 01:37:00.280
not used, by and large, for giant supply chain&nbsp;
shipments. Maybe there's a mismeasurement of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:00.280 --> 01:37:05.480
10% or 20% or something. But long story short,&nbsp;
I think it works out to about 1% of global GDP.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:05.480 --> 01:37:11.680
It's about a trillion dollars a year. As you say,&nbsp;
that then makes us really terrified of outages.&nbsp;

01:37:11.680 --> 01:37:18.240
And so we work so hard to enable fast iteration&nbsp;
and development cycles without having outages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:18.240 --> 01:37:25.280
and to put some numbers on it: we deploy&nbsp;
production services that are in the core&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:25.280 --> 01:37:33.920
charge flow around 1,000 times a day. Most of&nbsp;
these services are automatically deployed, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:33.920 --> 01:37:41.000
when anybody makes any production-ready change, it&nbsp;
just goes into production. It's meticulously and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:41.000 --> 01:37:46.520
carefully orchestrated: first is just running some&nbsp;
small sliver of traffic and then incrementally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:46.520 --> 01:37:54.680
more traffic until it's everything. So about&nbsp;
1,000 deploys per day at roughly or somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:54.680 --> 01:38:05.560
in excess of five-and-a-half-nines — 99.9995%&nbsp;
reliability — which works out to about two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:05.560 --> 01:38:10.400
two and a half minutes of unavailability per year.
It's not that we have, obviously, two and a half&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:10.400 --> 01:38:16.720
continuous minutes of unavailability, but that's&nbsp;
what it approximates to, even though it tends to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:16.720 --> 01:38:24.040
happen as background radiation throughout the&nbsp;
year. Getting to that point takes a huge amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:24.040 --> 01:38:31.560
of investment. Then there are security properties&nbsp;
that are less readily measured, but analogous to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:31.560 --> 01:38:41.120
those figures. Silicon Valley doesn't tend to...&nbsp;
I'm perhaps now being unfair in attributing things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:41.120 --> 01:38:46.960
to Silicon Valley — a lot of the tech industry&nbsp;
doesn't place a lot of value on process and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:46.960 --> 01:38:54.920
operational excellence. We culturally value the&nbsp;
spontaneous, the creative, the iconoclastic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:55.600 --> 01:39:08.640
the path-breaking. Building mechanisms that can&nbsp;
enable the very reliable provision of important&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:08.640 --> 01:39:18.680
services at scale, and removing the sources of&nbsp;
variability, that can really cause a bad day for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:18.680 --> 01:39:23.720
a very large number of people — I don't think&nbsp;
these things get quite as much cultural credit.&nbsp;

01:39:36.800 --> 01:39:41.000
None of this sounds like rocket science,&nbsp;
but defining what it is, that we care about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:41.000 --> 01:39:46.520
and then building automated measuring systems to&nbsp;
measure to what degree it's happening in practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:46.520 --> 01:39:49.480
to then try to figure out the cases where&nbsp;
we're not living up to that, and determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:49.480 --> 01:39:56.800
what is the reason, then to actually intervene and&nbsp;
improve the system, so that that's not happening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:56.800 --> 01:40:05.360
then importantly, to build secondary controls,&nbsp;
that detect instances of deviation long before&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:05.360 --> 01:40:11.440
they cause a production problem, but where&nbsp;
we understand the behavior of the system in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:11.440 --> 01:40:18.320
sufficient detail, so that we can instrument it in&nbsp;
some upstream way — most of what I said there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:18.320 --> 01:40:22.840
well understood by production engineers in 1930s.
So again, I'm not claiming that it's any kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:22.840 --> 01:40:29.680
radical breakthrough, but we have found that the&nbsp;
adoption of these practices in really tenacious&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:29.680 --> 01:40:41.480
multi-year form yields really high returns. There&nbsp;
may be other organizations that both ship at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:41.480 --> 01:40:52.360
rate and maintain that developer velocity at this&nbsp;
combination of scale and reliability and security,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:52.360 --> 01:40:58.200
but I don't think there are that many.&nbsp;
It's a real testament to the remarkable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:58.200 --> 01:41:04.320
folks at Stripe who made it happen.
Last point, the fact that you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:04.320 --> 01:41:10.600
this huge internal tooling and testing is...&nbsp;
Once you get the AI engineers, they can push&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:10.600 --> 01:41:15.000
the commits and you have the infrastructure&nbsp;
set up, so that it can be readily evaluated.&nbsp;

01:41:15.000 --> 01:41:27.200
Yeah. Across the board, so much comes back to what&nbsp;
has to be true for us to be able to build and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:27.200 --> 01:41:35.080
take seriously this goal of building the best&nbsp;
software. It's easy to say that as some lofty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:35.080 --> 01:41:42.560
vague, hand wavy aspirational statement. But if&nbsp;
you take that seriously as a goal — you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:42.560 --> 01:41:46.920
about what you would have to measure,&nbsp;
if you were actually going to pursue&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:46.920 --> 01:41:52.400
it in earnest? — part are the characteristics of&nbsp;
organizations that do produce it. You get down to:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:52.400 --> 01:41:58.400
"Well, customers have to really like your&nbsp;
stuff. OK, how can we measure that? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:58.400 --> 01:42:02.080
how can we systematize the process of making&nbsp;
sure that there aren't repressions there?"&nbsp;

01:42:02.080 --> 01:42:07.120
We have this concept of “experience journeys”,&nbsp;
which are pathways through Stripe, that we really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:07.120 --> 01:42:13.040
care about and that are always implemented at a&nbsp;
really high quality level. And it has to be true&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:13.040 --> 01:42:19.200
that developers can iterate over them very quickly&nbsp;
— we just spoke about how to make that happen etc.&nbsp;

01:42:21.040 --> 01:42:27.520
A theme through everything we've talked about&nbsp;
is taking the goal seriously. And I feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:27.520 --> 01:42:35.360
a lot of what we do at Stripe is — again,&nbsp;
I disclaim any genius in it — just the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:35.360 --> 01:42:41.800
earnest, repeated, serious, and long term&nbsp;
application of taking the goal seriously.&nbsp;

01:42:41.800 --> 01:42:49.000
A few more Stripe questions. One percent of&nbsp;
global GDP is such a staggering number. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:49.000 --> 01:42:54.520
you think about where further growth for Stripe&nbsp;
comes from, does it come from the internet economy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:54.520 --> 01:42:59.280
expanding? Or does it come from Stripe becoming&nbsp;
a larger share of the internet economy? And to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:59.280 --> 01:43:04.320
the extent that Stripe is growing faster than the&nbsp;
internet, if we consider that to be the beta in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:04.320 --> 01:43:13.720
your case, where is that alpha coming from?
That's a good question. The customers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:13.720 --> 01:43:23.600
Stripe serves in aggregate are outgrowing the&nbsp;
internet economy as a whole. At some point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:23.600 --> 01:43:28.240
those have to converge for obvious mathematical&nbsp;
reasons. But we're 14 years in and they haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:28.240 --> 01:43:33.520
converged yet. There's a lot of headroom there.&nbsp;
Say Stripe is handling around a trillion dollars a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:33.520 --> 01:43:39.960
year. When Stripe started out, the global economy&nbsp;
was sixty to seventy trillion-ish. The global&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:39.960 --> 01:43:48.280
economy is now around a hundred trillion. We still&nbsp;
have quite a bit of headroom before the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:48.280 --> 01:43:52.920
activity that is coming out to Stripe is really&nbsp;
butting up against the ceiling of global economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:52.920 --> 01:43:59.880
growth. And of course, there's no ceiling on&nbsp;
global economic growth for all sorts of reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:00.600 --> 01:44:07.160
It could be vastly higher than it is. And I don't&nbsp;
even mean new technologies or AIs, but just all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:07.160 --> 01:44:12.960
the basic per-capita math you can do around. What&nbsp;
if everybody had an income on par with the US?&nbsp;

01:44:15.480 --> 01:44:23.160
One of the reasons I am so interested in working&nbsp;
on Stripe is: it's the old line, the Lucas line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:23.160 --> 01:44:27.880
about how when you start thinking about&nbsp;
differential rates of development in countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:27.880 --> 01:44:31.680
it's hard to think about anything else. Why&nbsp;
does Brazil have the particular income and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:31.680 --> 01:44:36.400
GDP level that it does? Why does Poland have&nbsp;
the level that it does? Why did Ireland have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:36.400 --> 01:44:41.360
the trajectory that it did, where it went from&nbsp;
being the sick man of Europe to now one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:41.360 --> 01:44:45.800
wealthiest countries there? I feel like Stripe is&nbsp;
some applied version of this question in practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:45.800 --> 01:44:53.400
where you're building software products, but in&nbsp;
some sense connected to or touching upon these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:53.400 --> 01:45:03.000
questions of: Why aren't there more companies?&nbsp;
What determines the growth rate of a company?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:03.560 --> 01:45:12.760
Why is it that when you start the merch store,&nbsp;
why does it have X level of buyers rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:12.760 --> 01:45:26.840
2X? I think those remain fruitful questions.
We haven't optimized the meta system of business&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:26.840 --> 01:45:32.920
to any particularly great extent. For the vast&nbsp;
majority of time, businesses have been offline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:32.920 --> 01:45:43.440
inefficient, analog. It's really only&nbsp;
over the last one to two decades that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:43.440 --> 01:45:50.960
significant share of this has been meaningfully&nbsp;
digitized. And the prospects for optimizations&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:50.960 --> 01:45:57.360
there are still significantly underexplored.
We find incredibly basic things, like 'just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:58.960 --> 01:46:05.520
extending capital to businesses'. The reason we&nbsp;
do that is not to generate profit from the loans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:05.520 --> 01:46:08.680
but because we find that the businesses,&nbsp;
whom we extend the capital for, then just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:08.680 --> 01:46:18.280
grow faster on a persistent subsequent basis.
Or, trying to figure out, how does a business&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:18.280 --> 01:46:24.320
decide which countries it sells in? And you'll&nbsp;
find for even the smallest business through to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:24.320 --> 01:46:31.560
some of the largest businesses in the world,&nbsp;
that these are very ad hoc and not deeply&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:31.560 --> 01:46:35.480
thought through questions. Like: "Why don't you&nbsp;
sell in Mexico or in Brazil or whatever? — Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:35.480 --> 01:46:38.040
it seemed complicated, and so we&nbsp;
didn't quite get around to it."&nbsp;

01:46:41.800 --> 01:46:47.360
To your question about: where does the&nbsp;
growth come from? There's still an awful&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:47.360 --> 01:46:52.520
lot of low-hanging fruit in just asking&nbsp;
some of these incredibly basic questions.&nbsp;

01:46:52.520 --> 01:46:58.240
So, when we think about the way in which&nbsp;
Stripe will continue to grow in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:58.240 --> 01:47:03.680
in some sense, it will obviously involve a&nbsp;
lot of big businesses. You're now processing&nbsp;

01:47:03.680 --> 01:47:08.000
a significant amount of Amazon volume, there are&nbsp;
these other businesses you're doing deals with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:08.000 --> 01:47:12.880
First, tell me how you think. It makes sense&nbsp;
how an exponentially growing startup would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:12.880 --> 01:47:18.720
contribute to exponential growth for Stripe. How&nbsp;
does Stripe keep growing at the same trajectory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:18.720 --> 01:47:23.640
when it's these existing big businesses&nbsp;
that you're partnering with? And second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:24.840 --> 01:47:28.520
the case for why these startups matter is so&nbsp;
compelling, right? A new thing is coming into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:28.520 --> 01:47:32.840
this world, and we should really support it and&nbsp;
make sure it happens. Why is it compelling that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:32.840 --> 01:47:45.720
Amazon can fulfill orders more efficiently?
Those are very good questions. On the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:45.720 --> 01:47:59.680
one — you're right. Stripe is doomed to eventually&nbsp;
grow at the rate of the economy, there is only a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:59.680 --> 01:48:02.040
question of how long it takes to get there.
Right.&nbsp;

01:48:02.040 --> 01:48:08.480
The good news is that it can be a very long&nbsp;
time, because there is, as we just discussed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:08.480 --> 01:48:17.840
so much low hanging fruit around different&nbsp;
improvements that are possible. So I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:17.840 --> 01:48:23.240
it'll be many decades before that happens.&nbsp;
But it's true: that will eventually occur.&nbsp;

01:48:24.320 --> 01:48:33.200
On the second question: "It's obviously&nbsp;
virtuous or compelling or exciting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:33.200 --> 01:48:38.640
foster all these nascent startups and to be an&nbsp;
anti-incumbency force. But what's the case for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:39.720 --> 01:48:45.480
supporting established businesses?" People&nbsp;
misunderstand that for a small business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:45.480 --> 01:48:50.280
typically, at least in the cases where we&nbsp;
denote them startups, there's usually an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:50.280 --> 01:48:55.480
embedded innovation. And this innovation is all&nbsp;
that the company is. They have a new idea, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:55.480 --> 01:48:59.960
they're going to do something better or different&nbsp;
etc. Generally speaking, we like innovation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:59.960 --> 01:49:04.320
so we have positive sentiments towards that&nbsp;
startup. But there's a lot of innovation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:04.320 --> 01:49:09.680
comes from large established businesses. That's&nbsp;
not all they do, they are also just running the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:09.680 --> 01:49:16.600
existing thing. So maybe it's a smaller share,&nbsp;
but the aggregate fraction of innovation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:16.600 --> 01:49:24.400
comes from established businesses is really large.&nbsp;
We have to be cognizant of the cognitive bias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:24.400 --> 01:49:32.320
of the startups being more conspicuous. On a&nbsp;
relative basis, the improvements in turbine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:34.400 --> 01:49:44.640
fab or insulation technology come largely from&nbsp;
established businesses. To choose any sector&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:44.640 --> 01:49:49.840
of the economy, a significant fraction of the&nbsp;
important inventions that occurred over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:49.840 --> 01:49:58.120
10 or 20 years will have come from the incumbents.
As a general class, and Tyler wrote a book on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:58.120 --> 01:50:06.640
this, big business is underrated. If you look at&nbsp;
the survey data, people tend to have very positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:06.640 --> 01:50:12.187
sentiments not only towards startups, but towards&nbsp;
small business as a class. Even though they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:12.187 --> 01:50:16.440
have negative sentiments or relatively negative&nbsp;
sentiments towards big business, it is not that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:16.440 --> 01:50:23.120
bad on an absolute basis, but not as favorable.&nbsp;
It's true that established businesses tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:23.120 --> 01:50:27.280
pay better, tend to be more efficient, more&nbsp;
of the innovation in our economy comes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:27.280 --> 01:50:34.880
them and they produce a lot of consumer surplus.
The specific case for Stripe working with them is:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:34.880 --> 01:50:40.200
typically they're coming to us not because they&nbsp;
want to take the thing that they're already doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:40.200 --> 01:50:47.720
and go through all the work of transposing&nbsp;
to Stripe, but because either they want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:47.720 --> 01:50:52.760
do a new thing, that they're not doing today&nbsp;
— so it is associated with some new business&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:52.760 --> 01:51:03.320
line or innovation or invention. Or they've&nbsp;
spotted the opportunity to produce a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:03.320 --> 01:51:07.440
product and want to meaningfully change how they&nbsp;
provide an existing one in a fashion that, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:07.440 --> 01:51:11.960
yields consumer surplus. That sounds very&nbsp;
abstract and theoretical, but in practice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:11.960 --> 01:51:16.640
it tends to mean they want to take what they're&nbsp;
selling in this market and sell it in many more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:16.640 --> 01:51:20.960
markets. Or they've realized that they're&nbsp;
selling it in this modality, and they should&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:20.960 --> 01:51:27.800
sell it in other more convenient ways, like on&nbsp;
mobile or something. In each of those cases, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:27.800 --> 01:51:33.080
it's successful, if people buy it in significant&nbsp;
numbers, we're getting this decentralized signal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:33.080 --> 01:51:38.080
from the economy, that there's now something of&nbsp;
value being provided, that wasn't heretofore.&nbsp;

01:51:41.760 --> 01:51:49.680
As I take stock of the businesses, the enterprises&nbsp;
that are in the process of migrating to Stripe or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:49.680 --> 01:51:57.080
that did so over the last year, whether it's the&nbsp;
large retailers, global manufacturing firms or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:57.080 --> 01:52:06.000
shipping companies, it typically has one of those&nbsp;
two patterns. New product or current product sold&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:06.000 --> 01:52:10.680
to people who weren't buying it before.
Yeah. If you think about the big trends in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:10.680 --> 01:52:15.560
society that are needed to solve our big problems,&nbsp;
like Moore's Law or the cost of solar, you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:15.560 --> 01:52:20.400
marginal improvements over many decades.
Yes.&nbsp;

01:52:20.400 --> 01:52:23.720
Big tech or big companies are just able to&nbsp;
invest a lot of money into doing the R&amp;D.&nbsp;

01:52:23.720 --> 01:52:26.520
Relentless iterative improvement,&nbsp;
yes. It's underrated.&nbsp;

01:52:26.520 --> 01:52:28.280
Can I ask about John for a second?
Sure.&nbsp;

01:52:29.520 --> 01:52:32.880
You guys recently published Poor&nbsp;
Charlie's Almanac and subsequently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:32.880 --> 01:52:39.600
Charlie Munger has passed away. Did Munger&nbsp;
ever comment on your relationship and if or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:39.600 --> 01:52:47.800
whether it reminded him of his and Buffett's?
Not to me, but he knew John better. So it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:47.800 --> 01:52:56.360
possible that he did to John. Yeah, I don't know.
What have you learned about marriage from John?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:56.360 --> 01:53:03.960
This co-equal, intense, lengthy partnership — the&nbsp;
closest thing to that you have is marriage, right?&nbsp;

01:53:03.960 --> 01:53:09.880
Well, I'm relatively new to the practice of&nbsp;
marriage. So maybe in a decade I'll be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:09.880 --> 01:53:20.240
to extract the generalizable commonalities. The&nbsp;
general thing I'd say is: working with people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:20.240 --> 01:53:32.800
you're close to is underrated. I'm doing Arc with&nbsp;
Patrick Su and Silvana. Fast Grants was with Tyler&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:32.800 --> 01:53:40.520
and Silvana. Stripe is obviously with John. I&nbsp;
should mention, John was also instrumentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:40.520 --> 01:53:48.320
involved in Arc's formation. It would not have&nbsp;
happened without John. I could give more examples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:48.320 --> 01:53:55.920
but I feel like all the ventures of any&nbsp;
significance in my life, have not only been with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:55.920 --> 01:54:02.160
others, but been with other people that I'm very&nbsp;
close to. I had and would like to have an enduring&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:02.160 --> 01:54:08.160
relationship that outlives these ventures.
Sometimes one hears the advice that you shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:08.160 --> 01:54:15.200
work with friends, maybe you shouldn't work with&nbsp;
your partner or something like that. All these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:15.200 --> 01:54:22.920
things are idiosyncratic and there are instances&nbsp;
of every possible permutation. But for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:22.920 --> 01:54:29.000
it's been a really rewarding experience.&nbsp;
And I think John and I can work together&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:29.000 --> 01:54:40.320
for... You never know life, but I think we'll&nbsp;
probably work together for decades. For us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:40.320 --> 01:54:47.560
it's been both an important source of meaning&nbsp;
and, again, fulfillment, but also there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:47.560 --> 01:54:54.760
real complementarity. Stripe would be a less&nbsp;
effective company without either of us. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:54.760 --> 01:54:59.920
I'm just meaning from a bandwidth standpoint, but&nbsp;
I think we both bring different things to bear.&nbsp;

01:54:59.920 --> 01:55:02.880
Patrick, I think that's a great place to leave&nbsp;
it. Thank you so much for coming on the podcast.&nbsp;

01:55:02.880 --> 01:55:07.120
Thank you.
Hey, everybody! I hope you enjoyed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:07.120 --> 01:55:12.920
episode. As always, the most helpful thing you can&nbsp;
do is to share the podcast. Send it to people you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:12.920 --> 01:55:17.920
think might enjoy it. Put it on Twitter, your&nbsp;
group chats, etc. It just splits the world.&nbsp;

01:55:17.920 --> 01:55:31.280
Appreciate your listening. I'll see you next time.
Cheers.

